\h Leviticus
\toc1 The handbook of Sacred Living
\toc2 Leviticus
\mt2 The handbook of
\mt1 Sacred Living
\mt3 commonly called
\mt2 Leviticus
\rem This is still an early look into the unpolished text of the Open English Translation of the Bible. Please double-check the text in advance before using in public.
\c 1
\s1 Procedures for burnt sacrifices
\p
\v 1 Yahweh summoned Moses to the sacred tent and told him,
\v 2 “Tell the Israelites that when anyone presents an offering to Yahweh, it must be presented from their \add own\add* herd or flock.
\v 3 If it’s from the herd, it must be a bull with no defects. It must be presented in front of the entrance to the sacred tent to be acceptable to Yahweh.
\v 4 The owner must place his hand on the head of that offering to be burnt, and then it will be accepted make him right with Yahweh.
\v 5 Then he must slaughter the bull in front of Yahweh, and the priests (Aaron’s descendants), must present the blood and splash it all around the altar which is by the entrance of the sacred tent.
\v 6 Then the presenter must skin the bull and cut it into pieces,
\v 7 Meanwhile, the priest Aaron’s sons must put fire on the altar and arrange wood on the fire,
\v 8 and then they must arrange the pieces (including the head and the fat portions) on top of the burning wood on the altar.
\v 9 Then the presenter must wash the innards and the feet with water, and the priest must \add place it\add* on the altar, ensuring that every part is completely burnt up as a pleasing aroma for Yahweh.
\p
\v 10 “If the offering is from the flock (i.e., a sheep or a goat), then it must be a healthy male that’s presented,
\v 11 and then it must be slaughtered on the northern side of the altar facing Yahweh, and Aaron’s sons, the priests, must splash its blood on the altar, all around.
\v 12 Then the presenter must cut it into pieces and they must arrange them (including the head and fat portions) on top of the burning wood on the altar.
\v 13 Then the presenter must wash the innards and the feet with water, and the priest must \add place it\add* on the altar, ensuring that every part is completely burnt up as a pleasing aroma for Yahweh.
\p
\v 14 “However, if his burnt offering to Yahweh will be a bird, then it must be either a dove or a young pigeon.
\v 15 The priest must present it at the altar, and then twist its head off and burn the head on the altar as he drains the blood out onto the outer edge of the altar.
\v 16 Then he must pull out the crop with its contents, and throw it down on the eastern side of the altar where the ashes are.
\v 17 He will then tear the bird \add mostly\add* apart by its wings (but making sure that it stays as one piece), and \add place it\add* on the altar, ensuring that every part is completely burnt up as a pleasing aroma for Yahweh.
\c 2
\s1 Procedures for grain offerings
\p
\v 1 “When someone presents a grain offering to Yahweh, it must be as fine, wheat flour mixed with \add olive\add*, and with frankincense added to the top of it.
\v 2 It must be taken to the priest Aaron’s sons who will take it \add from them\add*, then take a handful out, being sure to get flour, oil, and some frankincense, and throw that memorial portion onto the altar to get completely burnt up as a pleasing aroma for Yahweh.
\v 3 Then the rest of that grain offering will be for Aaron and his sons—it’s a very sacred part from the gifts for Yahweh.
\p
\v 4 “When someone presents something baked as their grain offering, it must be baked without leaven: either wheat flour mixed with \add olive\add* oil and baked into flat buns, or else wheat flour biscuits with \add olive\add* oil spread on top.
\v 5 Or if your grain offering is done on the flat pan, it must be wheat flour mixed with \add olive\add* oil and made into flat biscuits
\v 6 that should then be crumbled and have oil poured over them as your grain offering.
\v 7 Or if your grain offering is \add deep-fried\add* in a pot, it must be made from wheat flour with \add olive\add* oil.
\p
\v 8 “\add Any of the above types of\add* grain offering to Yahweh must be presented to the priest, and he will take it to the altar.
\v 9 Then he must \add take a handful\add* as a memorial portion to the altar to get completely burnt up as a pleasing aroma for Yahweh.
\v 10 Then the rest of that grain offering will be for Aaron and his sons—it’s a very sacred part from the gifts for Yahweh.
\p
\v 11 “Anything that’s presented to Yahweh as a grain offering must not be made with any rising agent, because any leaven or honey mustn’t be burnt as a gift to Yahweh.
\v 12 Foods containing those can be presented as a first-fruits offering, but they mustn’t be burnt on the altar.
\v 13 However, every grain offering must be salted—the salt in the grain offering is a reminder of our agreement, so it must always be present.
\p
\v 14 “Finally, if you want to offer ripe grain as a grain offering to Yahweh, then it must be a young ear of grain that’s been roasted over the fire and presented as a grain offering of your first ripe grains
\v 15 with \add olive\add* oil on it and some frankincense on top. That \add also becomes\add* a grain offering,
\v 16 and the priest must burn a memorial portion from it with some of the crushed grains and some of the oil and some of the incense, \add to be burnt\add* to become smoke as a gift to Yahweh.
\c 3
\s1 Procedures for peace offerings
\p
\v 1 “Now if it’s a sacrifice for a peace offering, if it’s from the \bd herd\bd*, then either a male or female can be presented to Yahweh as long as it has no defects.
\v 2 The presenter must lay their hand on the head of the bull or cow, then he must slaughter it in front of the sacred tent. Then the priest Aaron’s sons will splash the blood on the altar on each side.
\v 3 From that peace offering, he must present the fat covering the innards and which is part of the innards as a gift to Yahweh,
\v 4 along with both kidneys and the fat that’s attached to them, and the lobe on the liver (all of which need to be removed \add from the animal\add*).
\v 5 Then Aaron’s sons must burn them on the wood that’s burning on the altar as a gift—to become smoke as a pleasing aroma for Yahweh.
\p
\v 6 “However, if the sacrifice for a peace offering is from the \bd flock\bd*, then either a male or a female can be presented to Yahweh as long as it has no defects.
\v 7 When the offering is being presented, it must be presented in front of Yahweh,
\v 8 and the presenter will put his hand on the head of \add the animal being\add* offered, and slaughter it in front of the sacred tent. Then Aaron’s sons must splash its blood on the altar, on each side.
\v 9 From that peace offering, he must present the fat as a gift to Yahweh, along with the entire fatty tail (cut off near the backbone), the fat covering the innards and which is part of the innards,
\v 10 both kidneys and the fat that’s attached to them, and the lobe on the liver (all of which need to be removed \add from the animal\add*).
\v 11 Then the priest must burn them on the altar as a food offering made to Yahweh by fire.
\p
\v 12 “Similarly, if the offering is a \bd goat\bd*, then it can be presented to Yahweh.
\v 13 The presenter will put his hand on its head, and slaughter it in front of the sacred tent. Then Aaron’s sons must splash its blood on the altar, on each side.
\v 14 From that peace offering, he must present the fat as a gift to Yahweh, along with the fat covering the innards and which is part of the innards,
\v 15 both kidneys and the fat that’s attached to them, and the lobe on the liver (all of which need to be removed \add from the animal\add*).
\v 16 Then the priest must burn them on the altar as a food offering made to Yahweh by fire as a pleasing aroma. All the fat belongs to Yahweh.
\p
\v 17 “\add This\add* regulation must be permanent for \add all\add* future generations: All of you must never eat fat or blood.”
\c 4
\s1 Procedures for sin offerings
\p
\v 1 Then Yahweh told Moses,
\v 2 “Tell the Israelites that if a person breaks any of Yahweh’s regulations unintentionally, or does anything that he said shouldn’t be done, \add then this is the procedure:\add*
\p
\v 3 “If it’s \bd the high priest\bd* who’s done it, \add that causes\add* the nation to be guilty, and for his transgression he must present a bull with no defects to Yahweh as a sin offering.
\v 4 He must bring the bull before Yahweh at the entrance of the sacred tent, then he must place his hand on its head and slaughter it in front of Yahweh.
\v 5 Then the high priest must get some of the bull’s blood and bringing it into the sacred tent,
\v 6 he must dip his finger into it and seven times he must flick the blood towards the front of the curtain of the sanctuary in front of Yahweh.
\v 7 Then the \add high\add* priest must put some of the blood on the \add four\add* projecting corners of the incense altar (located inside the \add sacred\add* tent in front of Yahweh \add and\add* used for \add burning\add* fragrant incense). Then \add he must go back outside and\add* pour the rest of the bull’s blood out at the base of the altar for burnt offerings (which is \add out\add* at the entrance to the sacred tent).
\v 8 Then the high priest must remove all the fat from the bull being offered as a sin offering, \add including\add* the fat covering the innards and which is a part of the innards,
\v 9 both kidneys and the fat that’s attached to them, and the lobe on the liver (all of which need to be removed \add from the animal\add*)
\v 10 just like you’d do for the bull of the peace offering. Then the priest must burn them on the altar for the burnt offering.
\v 11 However, the bull’s hide and its meat, plus its head and feet, and innards and dung,
\v 12 must be taken outside the camp to the clean place where they pour out the ashes, and they must \add make\add* a fire with wood there on top of the ashes and burn it there.
\p
\v 13 “If it’s the \bd whole community\bd* that \add unintentionally\add* transgresses by doing something that Yahweh prohibited, then they’ll be guilty even if the community didn’t \add actually\add* notice.
\v 14 When they become aware of what they’ve done wrong, then the community must present a young bull in front of the sacred tent as a sin offering.
\v 15 Then the community elders must place their hands of the bull’s head in front of Yahweh, and slaughter it there.
\v 16 The high priest must take some of the bull’s blood into the sacred tent
\v 17 and seven times he must dip his finger in it and flick the blood towards the front of the curtain of the sanctuary in front of Yahweh.
\v 18 Then he must put some of the blood on the \add four\add* projecting corners of the \add incense\add* altar (located inside the \add sacred\add* tent in front of Yahweh). Then \add he must go back outside and\add* pour the rest of the bull’s blood out at the base of the altar for burnt offerings (which is \add out\add* at the entrance to the sacred tent).
\v 19 Then he must remove all the fat from the slaughtered bull and burn up that fat on the altar,
\v 20 and he should process the bull just the same as for a sin offering. Then the priest will have made them right with God and they’ll be forgiven.
\v 21 Then he must take \add the remaining parts of\add* the bull outside the camp and burn it as described above \add and\add* it will be the sin offering for the community.
\p
\v 22 “If it’s \bd a leader\bd* that unintentionally transgresses by doing something that his god Yahweh prohibited, then he’ll be guilty.
\v 23 \add Alternatively, maybe someone else will point out his failing\add* to him. Then he must bring an offering of a male goat with no defects.
\v 24 He must place his hand on its head and slaughter it in the place where \add the animals for\add* the burnt offering are slaughtered. It will be a sin offering,
\v 25 and the priest must take some of the blood of that sin offering and with his finger, put some of the blood on the \add four\add* projecting corners of the altar for burnt offerings.
\v 26 He must burn up all the fat on the altar just like the fat from the peace offering, and \add that way\add*, the priest will make that leader right with God and he’ll be forgiven.
\p
\v 27 “If it’s \bd an \+add ordinary\+add* person\bd* who unintentionally transgresses by doing something that Yahweh prohibited, then they’ll be guilty.
\v 28 \add Alternatively, maybe someone else will point out their failing\add* to them. Then due to their transgression, that person must bring an offering of a female goat with no defects.
\v 29 He must place his hand on the head of the \add goat that’s a\add* sin offering, and slaughter it at the place for burnt offerings.
\v 30 Then the priest must take some of the blood \add and\add* with his finger, put some of the blood on the \add four\add* projecting corners of the altar for burnt offerings, and then pour \add the rest of\add* the blood at the altar’s base.
\v 31 Then the presenter must pull out all the fat (just like it’s done for the peace offering), and the priest will burn it on the altar to make a pleasing aroma for Yahweh, and to make that person right with God, and they’ll be forgiven.
\p
\v 32 “But if it’s a lamb that the individual wants to bring as their sin offering, then it must be a female with no defects.
\v 33 The presenter must put their hand on the head of the lamb and slaughter it as a sin offering at the place where burnt offerings are slaughtered.
\v 34 Then the priest must take some of the blood \add and\add* with his finger, put some of the blood on the \add four\add* projecting corners of the altar for burnt offerings, and then pour \add the rest of\add* the blood at the altar’s base.
\v 35 Then the presenter must pull out all the fat (just like it’s done for the peace offering), and the priest will burn it on the altar to make a pleasing aroma for Yahweh, and to make that person right with God, and they’ll be forgiven.
\c 5
\s1 More needs for sin offerings
\p
\v 1 “If someone is a witness to a case because of something they saw or knew about, and hears an oath \add in court\add* but doesn’t testify, then they’ll become guilty.
\p
\v 2 “If someone touches anything ‘unclean’ (e.g., a dead domestic or wild animal, or some creature) without realising it, they \add still\add* become ‘unclean’ and is guilty.
\p
\v 3 “If someone touches anything ‘unclean’ from \add another\add* person, even if they \add initially\add* didn’t realise what it was, they become guilty.
\p
\v 4 “If someone makes a rash promise, whether it’s to do something good or something bad, and even if they were \add just\add* speaking carelessly but find out \add later\add*, they become guilty.
\p
\v 5 “So then the person that became guilty from any of the above, must confess their wrongdoing
\v 6 and present a penalty to Yahweh to address that wrongdoing, and must present a female lamb or young goat as a sin offering, and the priest will \add act on their behalf\add* to make them right with God.
\p
\v 7 “However if that person can’t afford a sheep, then for their guilt offering because of their transgression, they must bring two doves or young pigeons to Yahweh—one for a sin offering and one for a burnt offering.
\v 8 The presenter must take them to the priest who will present the sin offering first—twisting the bird’s head off from the neck, but not pulling the body \add in half\add*.
\v 9 Then he must sprinkle some of the blood from that sin offering onto the side of the altar, and then drain out the rest of the blood at the altar’s base. That will be the sin offering.
\v 10 The second bird will be for a burnt offering as per the regulation. The priest will make the presenter right with God after their wrongdoing, and they’ll be forgiven.
\p
\v 11 “However if that person still can’t afford those two doves or young pigeons, then they can present a kilogram of fine wheat flour for a sin offering. It mustn’t have oil or frankincense in it because it’s a sin offering.
\v 12 The presenter must take it to the priest who will then take a handful of it, and burn that memorial portion on the altar for burnt offerings to Yahweh. That will be the sin offering.
\v 13 The priest will make the presenter right with God after their wrongdoing as per one of the above, and they’ll be forgiven. \add The rest of\add* the flour will be for the priest, similar to a grain offering.”
\s1 More on guilt offerings
\p
\v 14 Then Yahweh told Moses,
\v 15 “If someone acts unfaithfully but unintentionally desecrates something that’s sacred to Yahweh, then because of their guilt, that person must present ram with no defects from the flock, or silver coins using the official valuation, as a guilt offering.
\v 16 They must also compensate for the sacred item, but with an extra twenty percent. That must all be given to the priest who will make the presenter right with God with that ram as the guilt offering, and they’ll be forgiven.
\p
\v 17 “If someone transgresses by violating \add even\add* one of Yahweh’s regulations or prohibitions, even if they didn’t realise it, they become guilty and must bear responsibility for that guilt.
\v 18 Then that person must take a ram from the flock that has no defects to the priest for a guilt offering. Then the priest will make the presenter right with God after that transgression (even if they weren’t aware of it), and they’ll be forgiven.
\v 19 That’s a guilt offering, because they were clearly guilty before Yahweh.”
\c 6
\p
\v 1 Then Yahweh told Moses,
\v 2 “Someone who transgresses against Yahweh by cheating with respect to a deposit or a pledge, or by theft or extortion,
\v 3 or by finding lost property and then denying it, or making an oath that was knowingly false, or by any other typical transgression that people do,
\v 4 then they’ve become guilty as a result of that transgression. That person must return what was stolen or extorted, or return the deposit or the lost property,
\v 5 or make amends for a false oath. They must return the full value plus an extra twenty percent as soon as the guilt is discovered.
\v 6 Then that person must take a ram from the flock with no defects to the priest as a guilt offering to Yahweh,
\v 7 and the priest will make the presenter right with Yahweh after whatever that transgression was to make them guilty, and they’ll be forgiven.
\s1 The fire and the ashes
\p
\v 8 Then Yahweh told Moses,
\v 9 “Tell Aaron and his sons that these are the regulations for the burnt offerings: The burnt offering must remain on the altar’s hearth, on the altar all night until the morning and the fire must be kept burning on it.
\v 10 Then the priest must put on his linen underwear and linen robe and remove the fatty ashes from the burnt offerings on the altar, and pile them beside it.
\v 11 After that, he’ll get changed again into his regular clothes and take those ashes outside the camp to a ‘clean’ place.
\v 12 Meanwhile, the fire on the altar must be kept burning—it must never go out. Every morning the priest must put more wood on the fire, then he must arrange more offerings on the fire—he should burn the fat of the fellowship offerings on that fire so the smoke rises.
\v 13 \add Yes,\add* the fire must burn continually on the altar—never going out.
\s1 Procedures for the grain offering
\p
\v 14 “Here’s the regulations for the grain offering: Aaron’s sons must present it before Yahweh in front of the altar.
\v 15 The priest must take a handful of the flour from that grain offering, being sure it includes some of the oil and the incense, and burn it on the altar as a memorial portion to become a pleasing aroma for Yahweh.
\v 16 The remainder \add of that flour\add* will be used as food for Aaron and his sons—they must eat the flat bread in a sacred place, i.e., in the courtyard of the sacred tent.
\v 17 It must not be baked with any rising agent. I give that to them from what’s given to me. It’s very sacred just like the sin offering and the guilt offering.
\v 18 Any of Aaron’s male descendants can eat it—it’ll be their portion from Yahweh’s gifts throughout the generations. Anyone who touches Yahweh’s gifts will become sacred \add themself\add*.”
\s1 Procedures for the ordination offering
\p
\v 19 Then Yahweh told Moses,
\v 20 “On the day when one of Aaron’s male descendants is consecrated \add as a priest\add*, this is what he must present to Yahweh: a continual grain offering of a kilogram of fine wheat flour—half for the morning and half for the evening.
\v 21 It must be mixed with \add olive\add* oil then cooked with oil on a flat pan. It must then be broken into pieces and presented as a grain offering to make a pleasing aroma for Yahweh.
\v 22 The priest who’s next in line to become high priest must offer it and it must be completely burnt for Yahweh. That is a permanent regulation.
\v 23 Any grain offering by a priest must be completely burnt—it must not be eaten.”
\s1 Procedures for the sin offering
\p
\v 24 Then Yahweh told Moses,
\v 25 “Tell Aaron and his sons that these are the regulations for the sin offerings: The sin offering must be slaughtered in front of Yahweh at the place where the burnt offerings are slaughtered. It’s a very sacred \add activity\add*.
\v 26 The priest who offers the sin offering must be the one to eat it. It must be eaten in a sacred place, i.e., in the courtyard of the sacred tent.
\v 27 Everything that touches the \add animal’s\add* body will become sacred. Anyone who gets the blood on their clothes, must wash them in a sacred place.
\v 28 Any clay pot that it’s been boiled in, must then be shattered, but if it’s boiled in a bronze pot, then that must be scoured and rinsed with water.
\v 29 Any male in the priests’ \add families\add* may eat it—it’s very sacred.
\v 30 However, every sin offering that has its blood taken inside the sacred tent to make someone right with God, then that \add meat\add* mustn’t be eaten—it must be completely burnt up.
\c 7
\s1 Procedures for the guilt offering
\p
\v 1 “Then these are the regulations for the very sacred guilt offerings:
\v 2 The guilt offering must be slaughtered at the same place as the burnt offerings, and the blood must be splashed on each side of the altar.
\v 3 The presenter must present all the fat \add including\add* the fatty tail, the fat covering the innards,
\v 4 both kidneys and the fat that’s attached to them, and the lobe on the liver (all of which need to be removed \add from the animal\add*).
\v 5 Then the priest must burn those on the altar as a guilt offering to Yahweh.
\v 6 Any male in the priests’ \add families\add* may eat it—it’s very sacred.
\v 7 The regulations are the same for the guilt offering as for the sin offering—the priest who offers the guilt offering must be the one to eat it.
\p
\v 8 “Whenever a priest presents someone’s burnt offering, the hide of the animal that was presented belongs to that priest.
\v 9 Any grain offering that’s baked in an oven, or cooked in a pan or on a griddle, will belong to the priest who presents it.
\v 10 Any grain offering that’s either dry or mixed with oil, belongs to all of Aaron’s male descendants without any distinction.
\s1 Procedures for the peace offering
\p
\v 11 “Then these are the regulations for sacrificing peace offerings to present to Yahweh:
\v 12 If it’s presented from thankfulness, then the sacrifice must be accompanied by thanksgiving flat bread buns (\add made from flour\add* mixed with \add olive\add* oil), and flat biscuits smeared with oil, and buns \add made from\add* fine wheat flour mixed with oil,
\v 13 plus risen bread rolls. \add All that\add* must accompany the peace offering from thankfulness.
\v 14 Then he must present one of each \add kind of bread\add* as a contribution to Yahweh. Those will belong to the priest who splashes the blood for the peace offerings.
\v 15 The meat of the thankfulness sacrifice of those peace offerings must be eaten the same day that it’s offered—none of it can be left until morning.
\v 16 However, if the sacrificed offering is a vow or freewill offering, then it can be eaten that same day and \add also\add* the next day,
\v 17 but any left-over meat must be burnt up on the fire on the third day.
\v 18 If any of that meat from the sacrifice was eaten on the third day, the presenter won’t be accepted and they won’t be forgiven. It is spoilt, and anyone who eats any of it will bear their own guilt.
\v 19 If any meat touches anything that’s ‘unclean’, then it must not be eaten—it must be burnt up in the fire. However, any long as the meat remains ‘clean’, it can be eaten by any person who’s ‘clean’.
\v 20 If a person who was ‘unclean’ were to eat any of the meat from the sacrifice for a peace offering, then that person must be cut off from the community.
\v 21 Similarly, when a person touches anything ‘unclean’ (e.g., human uncleanness, or an unclean animal, or a detestable creature), and then they eat some of the meat of the sacrifice for a peace offering that \add now\add* belongs to Yahweh, that person must be cut off from the community.
\s1 Don’t eat fat or blood
\p
\v 22 Then Yahweh told Moses,
\v 23 “Tell the Israelites that you all must not eat the fat from cattle or sheep or goats,
\v 24 plus you all definitely must not eat the fat from an animal that died naturally or was killed by wild animals although you can use it for other purposes.
\v 25 Anyone who does eat the fat from an animal presented as a gift to Yahweh must be cut off from the community.
\p
\v 26 “Also, you all must not eat blood from either a bird or an animal, no matter where you’re living.
\v 27 Any person who does eat blood must be cut off from the community.”
\s1 The priests’ portions
\p
\v 28 Then Yahweh told Moses,
\v 29 “Tell the Israelites that anyone who offers a peace offering to Yahweh must take some of it as his peace offering to Yahweh.
\v 30 They must personally carry Yahweh’s gift \add which will be\add* the breast with its fat and raise it in front of Yahweh as a wave offering,
\v 31 and the priest must burn that fat up on the altar but the breast will be for Aaron and his sons.
\v 32 The right thigh from the sacrifices of your peace offerings must be given to the priest—
\v 33 whichever priest (Aaron’s descendant) who handled the blood of the peace offerings and the fat, that right thigh will be his portion
\v 34 because I took the breast from the wave offering and the thigh from the contribution of the Israelites’ peace offerings, and I gave them to Aaron the priest and his sons as an ongoing portion from the Israelite people.
\v 35 That’s the share for Aaron and his sons from the gifts to Yahweh since Moses presented them to Yahweh as priests.
\v 36 Yahweh commanded them to be given to them \add back\add* on the day when he anointed them out of all the Israelite people \add and made it\add* a permanent regulation for \add future\add* generations.
\p
\v 37 Those are the regulations for the burnt offerings, the grain offerings, the sin offerings, the guilt offerings, the ordination offerings, and the peace offerings
\v 38 that Yahweh gave to Moses at Mt. Sinai on the day when he ordered the Israelites to present their offerings to Yahweh \add when they were there\add* in the Sinai wilderness.
\c 8
\s1 Ordination of Aaron and his sons
\r (Exo. 29:1-37)
\p
\v 1 Then Yahweh told Moses,
\v 2 “Take Aaron and his sons with their clothes and the anointing oil, and the bull for the sin offering and two rams, and a basket of unleavened bread,
\v 3 and assemble the entire community at the entrance to the sacred tent.”
\p
\v 4 So Moses did exactly has Yahweh had commanded him, and the people assembled at the entrance of the sacred tent.
\v 5 Then Moses told the assembled people, “This \add ceremony\add* is what Yahweh has told us to do.”
\p
\v 6 Then Moses presented Aaron and his sons and \add after\add* washing them with water,
\v 7 he put the tunic on Aaron and wrapped the sash \add around\add* him, and then put the robe on him. Then he put the sacred apron on him, tying its finely woven waistband to fasten it onto him.
\v 8 Then he attached the chest pouch and put the Urim and Tumim into the pouch.
\v 9 Finally he placed the turban on his head, and fastened the golden flower-shaped plate (that sacred crown \add that demonstrates that Aaron is set apart to only serve Yahweh\add*) to the front of the turban, exactly as Yahweh had instructed Moses.
\p
\v 10 Then Moses took the anointing oil and anointed Yahweh’s residence and everything in it, thus consecrating it all.
\v 11 Seven times he flicked some of it onto the altar—anointing the altar and all its utensils and the basin and its base, thus consecrating them.
\v 12 He poured some of the anointing oil over Aaron’s head, thus anointing him to consecrate him.
\v 13 Then Moses presented Aaron’s sons and dressed them in tunics and wrapped sashes around each of them, and tied their headbands onto them, just as Yahweh had instructed Moses.
\p
\v 14 Then he brought the bull for the sin offering, and Aaron and his sons placed their hands on its head.
\v 15 Then Aaron\f + \fr 8:15 \ft It’s not 100% clear from the Hebrew if the pronoun here refers to Aaron or to Moses, but as Moses’s name is specifically mentioned in the next clause, we assume that ‘he’ was still referring to Aaron here.\f* slaughtered it, and Moses took the blood and used his finger to put some on the \add four\add* projecting corners of the altar, thus purifying the altar. Then he poured \add the rest of\add* the blood out at the altar’s base, sanctifying it so it could be used for making people right with God.
\v 16 Then Moses took all the far that was on the innards and the lobe of the liver, and the two kidneys and their fat, and he burnt them up on the altar.
\v 17 However, he burnt up \add the rest of\add* the bull including its hide, flesh, and dung, outside the camp, just as Yahweh had told him.
\p
\v 18 Then Moses presented the ram for the burnt offering, and Aaron and his sons placed their hands on its head.
\v 19 Then Aaron\f + \fr 8:19 \ft It’s not 100% clear from the Hebrew if the pronoun here refers to Aaron or to Moses, but as Moses’s name is specifically mentioned in the next clause, we assume that ‘he’ was still referring to Aaron here.\f* slaughtered it, and Moses splashed the blood on each side of the altar.
\v 20 Then Aaron\f + \fr 8:20 \ft Again it’s not 100% clear from the Hebrew if the pronoun here refers to Aaron or to Moses, but as Moses’s name is specifically mentioned in the next clause, we assume that ‘he’ was referring to Aaron (and possibly including his sons) here.\f* cut the ram into pieces, and Moses burnt up the head, and the pieces and the fat.
\v 21 However, Aaron\f + \fr 8:20 \ft Again it’s not 100% clear from the Hebrew if the pronoun here refers to Aaron or to Moses, but as Moses’s name is specifically mentioned in the next clause, we assume that ‘he’ was referring to Aaron here.\f* washed the innards and the legs, then Moses \add also\add* burnt those up on the altar as a burnt offering that was pleasing aroma for Yahweh, just as Yahweh had instructed Moses.
\p
\v 22 Then Moses presented the second ram for the ordination offering, and Aaron and his sons placed their hands on the ram’s head.
\v 23 Then Aaron\f + \fr 8:19 \ft It’s not 100% clear from the Hebrew if the pronoun here refers to Aaron or to Moses, but as Moses’s name is specifically mentioned in the next clause, we assume that ‘he’ was still referring to Aaron here.\f* slaughtered it, and Moses took some of its blood and put it on the lobe of Aaron’s right ear, on his right thumb, and on his right big toe.
\v 24 Then he presented Aaron’s sons and put some of the blood on their right earlobes, and their right thumbs, and their right big toes. Then Moses splashed the blood on each side of the altar.
\v 25 He collected the fat and the fatty tail, and all the fat that was on the innards and the lobe of the liver, and the two kidneys with their fat, and the right thigh,
\v 26 Then from the basket of unleavened bread that was there in front of Yahweh, he took one flat bread bun, and one flat bread that had been smeared with oil, and one flat biscuit, and placed them on \add top of\add* the fat and the right thigh.
\v 27 He place all that onto the open hands of Aaron and his sons who raised them as a wave offering in front of Yahweh.
\v 28 Then Moses took it all again, and burnt it all up on the altar with the burnt offering. That ordination offering on the fire became a pleasing aroma for Yahweh.
\v 29 Then Moses took the breast \add meat\add* from the ram for the ordination offering, and raised it as a wave offering in front of Yahweh, just as Yahweh had instructed him.
\p
\v 30 Then Moses took some of the anointing oil and some of the blood from on the altar, and he flicked them onto Aaron and his clothes, his sons and their clothes, thus consecrating Aaron and his clothes, and his sons and their clothes.
\p
\v 31 Then Moses told Aaron and his sons, “Boil the meat at the entrance to the sacred tent and eat it, along with the \add flat\add* bread that’s in the basket as part of the ordination offering, just as I had instructed that Aaron and his sons should eat it.
\v 32 Any meat or bread that’s leftover must be burnt in the fire.
\v 33 None of you may leave the entrance to the sacred tent for seven days. \add That’s because\add* Yahweh will ordain you all over those seven days.
\v 34 What’s been done today has been initiated by Yahweh in order to make you all right with him,
\v 35 so remain here day and night at the entrance of the sacred tent for seven days as per Yahweh’s instructions and you all won’t die, because that’s what I’ve been commanded.”
\v 36 So Aaron and his sons did everything that Yahweh had instructed them via Moses.
\c 9
\s1 The priests start their duties
\p
\v 1 On the eighth day, Moses summoned Aaron and his sons, along with Israel’s elders,
\v 2 and he told Aaron, “Get a healthy, young, male calf for a sin offering for yourself, and a ram without defects for a burnt offering, and present them to Yahweh.
\v 3 Then tell the Israelite people to take a male goat for a sin offering, and a calf and a lamb, both a year old and without defects for a burnt offering,
\v 4 as well as a bull and a ram to sacrifice to Yahweh as peace offerings, and a grain offering mixed will oil, because Yahweh will appear to you all today.”
\p
\v 5 So they brought \add everything\add* to the sacred tent that Moses had instructed them, and the whole assembly presented them as they stood before Yahweh,
\v 6 then Moses said, “You’ve done what Yahweh told you, so now you’ll all see his brightness appear.”
\v 7 Then he told Aaron, “Go to the altar and make your sin offering and your burnt offering to make yourself and the people right with God. Then make the people’s offering to make them right with God, just as Yahweh commanded.”
\p
\v 8 So Aaron approached the altar and slaughtered the calf for his own sin offering,
\v 9 and his sons presented the blood to him. Dipping his finger into the blood, he smeared it on the altar’s \add four\add* projecting corners, then he poured \add the rest of\add* the blood out at the altar’s base.
\v 10 He burnt up the fat and the kidneys, and the lobe from the liver for the sin offering on the altar, just as Yahweh had instructed Moses,
\v 11 but the meat and the skin, he burnt up in a fire outside the camp.
\p
\v 12 Then Aaron slaughtered the burnt offering, and his sons brought the blood to him and he splashed it on each side of the altar.
\v 13 Then they brought him the head and the \add cut-up\add* pieces, and he burnt them up on the altar.
\v 14 He washed the innards and the legs, and then \add also\add* burnt them up on the altar.
\p
\v 15 Then Aaron presented the people’s offering. He took the goat for the people’s offering and slaughtered it, then offered it as a sin offering like the other one.
\v 16 He presented the burnt offering—following all the instructions when he did it.
\v 17 Then he presented the grain offering, and taking a large handful, he burnt it on the altar, in addition to the morning’s burnt offering.
\v 18 Then Aaron slaughtered the bull and the ram \add which\add* were the people’s peace offering, and his sons brought the blood to him and he splashed it on each side of the altar.
\v 19 He took the fat from the bull and the ram, including their fatty tails and the fat covering the innards, the kidneys, and the lobe of the liver,
\v 20 and placed it \add all\add* on top of those animals breast \add meat\add*, then burnt up the fat on the altar.
\v 21 Finally, he raised the breast \add meat\add* and the right thigh as a wave offering \add in front\add* of Yahweh, just as Moses had instructed.
\p
\v 22 Then Aaron raised his arms towards the people and blessed them, and then he stepped down, \add having finished\add* making the sin offering, the burnt offering, and the peace offerings.
\v 23 Moses and Aaron entered the sacred tent, and \add when\add* they came \add back\add* out, they blessed the people and Yahweh’s brilliance appeared to all the people.
\v 24 Then flames shot out from Yahweh’s front and evaporated the burnt offering and the fat on the altar. They people saw that \add happen\add*, and they yelled out and fell down \add onto their knees\add* with their faces to the ground.
\c 10
\s1 Nadav and Avihu get destroyed
\p
\v 1 Then two of Aaron’s sons, Nadav and Avihu each took their incense-burning pans and put fire in them, and put incense on the fire, thus offering unauthorised fire to Yahweh that he hadn’t instructed them to do.
\v 2 So flames shot out from Yahweh’s front and consumed then so they died \add there\add* in front of Yahweh.
\v 3 Then Moses told Aaron, “That’s what Yahweh meant when he said, ‘I will reveal my holiness to those who approach me, then I’ll be honoured in the sight of all the people.’ ”
\p
\v 4 Then Moses summoned Mishael and Elitsafan, the sons of Aaron’s Uncle Uziel, and told them, “Come and help by carrying your cousins’ \add bodies\add* from the sacred place \add here\add* to a place outside the camp.”
\v 5 So they came and carried the two corpses by their clothes to a place outside the camp just as Moses had told them to do.
\p
\v 6 Then Moses told Aaron and his sons Eleazar and Itamar, “Don’t leave your hair loose, and don’t tear your clothes, so that you won’t die and so that Yahweh won’t be angry at the entire community. But meanwhile, all the Israelites must mourn because Yahweh was angry enough to burn \add those two\add*.
\v 7 Also, don’t leave the entrance to the sacred tent, as you might die because you all \add still\add* have Yahweh’s anointing oil on you.” So they did everything that Moses said.
\s1 Instructions for priests
\p
\v 8 Then Yahweh told Aaron,
\v 9 “\add Neither\add* you or your sons should drink wine or strong drink when you’re \add going to\add* go into the sacred tent so that you won’t die. This regulation is permanent and for future generations.
\v 10 You must \add carefully\add* distinguish between what’s sacred and what’s common, and what’s ‘unclean’ and ‘clean’.
\v 11 and you must teach the Israelites all the instructions that Yahweh has given them via Moses.”
\p
\v 12 Then Moses told Aaron and his remaining sons Eleazar and Itamar, “Take the remainder of the grain offering after \add the memorial portion has been given\add* to Yahweh, and eat that flat bread \add there\add* beside the altar, because it’s very sacred.
\v 13 You all must eat it in a sacred place, because it’s the portion for you and your sons from the gifts to Yahweh, because that’s what I’ve been instructed.
\v 14 Also, you all must eat the breast \add meat\add* from the wave offering and the thigh from the contribution \add offering\add* in a ‘clean’ place. Your sons and daughters can join you all for that, because that food has been given as the portion for you and your children from the peace offering sacrifices from the Israelites.
\v 15 \add Yes,\add* those thighs from the contribution \add offering\add* and the breast \add meat\add* from the wave offering, in addition to the gifts of the fat portions, must be brought before Yahweh as a raised offering, and then they’ll always become the portion for you and your families, just as Yahweh instructed.”
\p
\v 16 Then Moses looked for the goat \add meat\add* from the sin offering, but was shocked to discover that it had been burnt up \add on the altar\add*, so he was angry with Aaron’s remaining sons Eleazar and Itamar, telling them,
\v 17 “Why didn’t you eat that sin offering in the sacred place, because it’s very sacred and he gave it to you to bear the community’s guilt then to make them right with Yahweh.
\v 18 Listen here! Its blood \em wasn’t\em* taken into the inner sacred place, \add so\add* you must eat it in the sacred place just like I told you to do!”
\p
\v 19 Then Aaron explained to Moses, “Listen, they presented their sin offering and their burnt offering to Yahweh today, and yet I lost two of my sons. But if I’d eaten the sin offering today, would it have pleased Yahweh?”
\v 20 When Moses heard that, he was satisfied.
\c 11
\s1 Creatures that may be eaten
\r (Deu. 14:3-21)
\p
\v 1 Then Yahweh told Moses and Aaron:
\p
\v 2 Tell the Israelites that the following creatures on the earth are the ones that you all can eat:
\v 3 You can all eat any animal that has hooves which are completely split in two and that \add also\add* chews the cud.
\v 4 However you mustn’t eat those without \+em both\+em* traits, for example camels chew the cud but don’t have divided hooves, \add so\add* they are ‘unclean’ for you.
\v 5 The rock badger likewise, because although it chews the cud, it \add too\add* doesn’t have divided hooves,
\v 6 and the rabbit is ‘unclean’ for you all because although it chews the cud, it doesn’t have divided hooves.
\v 7 Pigs have divided hooves that are split completely, but don’t chew the cud, \add so\add* they’re ‘unclean’ for you all—
\v 8 you mustn’t eat their meat or touch their carcasses \add because\add* they’re ‘unclean’ for you all.
\p
\v 9 You all can eat everything that \add lives\add* in the water, \add whether\add* the oceans or rivers, as long as it has \add both\add* fins and scales.
\v 10 In contrast, anything in the oceans or rivers without \add both\add* fins and scales, whether they’re swarming creatures or otherwise, they’ll \add all\add* be detestable for you all.
\v 11 So anything that’s detestable to you all, you mustn’t eat their meat and you must detest their carcasses.
\v 12 Anything in the water that doesn’t have \add both\add* fins and scales will be detestable for you all.
\p
\v 13 From the birds, the ones that you’ll all detest and avoid eating will include: the eagle and any kind of vulture,
\v 14 the kite and any kind of falcon,
\v 15 any kind of crow,
\v 16 any kind of owl, gull, or hawk,
\v 17 the cormorant,
\v 18 the osprey,
\v 19 the stork, any kind of heron, the hoopoe, and bats.
\v 20 Everything that swarms with wings and walks on four \add legs\add* must be detestable for you all.
\v 21 However, you all can eat anything that swarms with wings and walks on four \add legs\add* that has its legs above its feet for hopping on the ground.
\v 22 You all can eat any kind of locust or cricket or grasshopper,
\v 23 but anything that swarms with wings and walks on four \add legs\add* must be detestable for you all.
\p
\v 24 The following will make you ‘unclean’—anyone who touches their carcasses will become ‘unclean’ until the evening,
\v 25 plus anyone who carries their carcass must wash their clothes and will be ‘unclean’ until the evening:
\v 26 Any animal with a divided hoof but not completely split in two or that doesn’t chew the cud will be ‘unclean’ for you all—anyone who touches them will become ‘unclean’.
\v 27 Also, any four-footed animal that walks on its paws is ‘unclean’ for you—anyone who touches their carcass will become ‘unclean’ until the evening,
\v 28 plus anyone who carries their carcass must wash their clothes and will be ‘unclean’ until the evening \add because\add* they’re ‘unclean’ for you all.
\p
\v 29 These are the ‘unclean’ creatures from the ones that swarm on the ground: rats, mice, lizards
\v 30 of any kind, geckos, skinks, and chameleons.
\v 31 Out of all the swarming things, those are ‘unclean’ for you—anyone who touches them when they’ve died will be ‘unclean’ until the evening.
\v 32 Also, anything that their dead bodies fall on will become ‘unclean’, whether it’s a wooden, cloth, leather, or \add coarse\add* sackcloth object, or any container used for work, \add so then\add* it must be immersed in water and it will be ‘unclean’ until the evening when it will become ‘clean’ \add again\add*.
\v 33 \add If\add* one of those creatures falls into a clay container, everything in it will become ‘unclean’ and you must break that container.
\v 34 If water from \add that container\add* got onto any food, it will become ‘unclean’, and any drink take from that container will be ‘unclean’.
\v 35 Anything that the dead body of one of those creatures falls onto will become ‘unclean’—any oven or stove must be smashed. They have become ‘unclean’ and would always remain so.
\v 36 However a spring or a cistern for collecting water will be ‘clean’, but anyone touching that dead body will be ‘unclean’.
\v 37 If the dead body fell on some seeds that will be planted, that seed is still ‘clean’,
\v 38 but if the seeds are wet when the dead body falls on it, then it’s ‘unclean’ for you.
\p
\v 39 If any animal that you all can eat dies, then any person touches its carcass will be ‘unclean’ until the evening.
\v 40 Anyone who eats any \add part\add* of that dead body, and anyone who moves it, must wash their clothes and will be ‘unclean’ until the evening.
\p
\v 41 Every creature that swarms along the ground is detestable—it mustn’t be eaten.
\v 42 Everything that \add moves\add* on its belly, and any swarming thing that moves along the ground on four or more feet mustn’t be eaten because they’re detestable.
\v 43 Don’t make yourselves detestable with any of those swarming creatures, and don’t make yourselves ‘unclean’ by \add touching or eating\add* them,
\v 44 because I am your god Yahweh. You all must keep yourselves holy, and you’re a holy people because I’m holy. So don’t make yourselves ‘unclean’ with any swarming creatures that creep across the ground
\v 45 because I am Yahweh, the one who brought you all out of Egypt to be your god, and you must be holy because I am holy.
\p
\v 46 The \add above\add* are the regulations about livestock, birds, creatures that move in the water, and creatures that swarm across the ground,
\v 47 to distinguish between the ‘unclean’ and the ‘clean’, i.e., those that can be eaten and those that must not be eaten.
\c 12
\s1 Purification after giving birth
\p
\v 1 Then Yahweh told Moses:
\p
\v 2 Tell the Israelites that when a woman gets pregnant and then gives birth to a boy, she will then be ‘unclean’ for seven days similar to how her impurity during her monthly period makes her ‘unclean’.
\v 3 Then on the eighth day, the baby boy’s foreskin must be circumcised.
\v 4 The mother will remain impure from the blood for thirty-three days—she must not touch any sacred thing and may not enter the sanctuary until her purification time is completed.
\p
\v 5 However, if she gave birth to a girl, she’ll be ‘unclean’ for two weeks similar to how her impurity during her monthly period, and her purification time will be sixty-six days.
\p
\v 6 When the mother’s purification time is completed for either a son or a daughter, she must take a one-year-old lamb as a burnt offering and a young pigeon or dove as a sin offering to the priest at the entrance to the sacred tent.
\v 7 Then he must present them to Yahweh to make her right with God, and then she’ll be purified from her flow of blood. That’s the regulation for a woman who gives birth to either a male or a female.
\p
\v 8 If she’s unable to afford a lamb, then she must take \em two\em* young pigeons or doves—one for the burnt offering and one for the sin offering. Then the priest will make her right with God, and then she’ll will have been purified.
\c 13
\s1 Regulations about skin diseases
\p
\v 1 Then Yahweh told Moses and Aaron:
\p
\v 2 If \add someone discovers\add* a raised area on their skin, or a scab or a bright spot, and it seems like an infectious skin disease, then that person should be taken to a priest (Aaron or one of his sons),
\v 3 and the priest must examine their skin. If the hair in the infected \add spot\add* has turned white and the infection appears to be deeper under the skin, then it’s \add confirmed as\add* an infectious skin disease and so the priest must pronounce the person as ‘unclean’.
\v 4 \add However,\add* if it’s a white spot that doesn’t seem to be deep, and the hair hasn’t turned white, then the priest \add must order\add* the person to be quarantined for seven days.
\v 5 Then on the seventh day, if the infection is still there but hasn’t spread, then the priest \add must order\add* a further seven days of quarantine.
\v 6 After that next seven days, if the infection has faded and not spread, the priest must pronounce the person as ‘clean’—it was just a sore so the person must wash their clothes and then they’ll be ‘clean’.
\v 7 However, if the spot does spread across the skin \em after\em* the priest has pronounced the person to be ‘clean’, then they must return to the priest a second time.
\v 8 The priest must re-examine that person and if the spot on the skin has spread, then the priest must pronounce them ‘unclean’ because they have a skin disease.
\p
\v 9 Anyone who develops an infectious skin disease must be taken to a priest
\v 10 to be examined. If it’s a white spot on the skin and the hair has turned white, and there’s raw flesh \add visible\add* in the swelling,
\v 11 then it’s a chronic skin disease, and the priest must pronounce that person as ‘unclean’ and can’t just be \add temporarily\add* quarantined.
\v 12 However, if the disease has broken out and spread across the entire body from head to toe as far as can be observed by the priest,
\v 13 then \add after\add* the priest’s confirmation that it covers the entire body, that person can be pronounced as ‘clean’ \add again\add* as all of it has turned white.
\v 14 However, if any raw flesh shows itself, then they’ll become ‘unclean’ that same day.
\v 15 The priest must examine that raw flesh and must pronounce that person as ‘unclean’ due to their skin disease.
\v 16 Afterwards, if it heals over and goes white, then they can return to the priest
\v 17 to be reexamined, and if the infection has indeed gone white, then the priest can pronounce it as not being infectious and the person as ‘clean’ \add again\add*.
\p
\v 18 When someone has a boil on their skin, but it heals
\v 19 leaving behind a white swelling or a reddish-white bright spot, then that person must show themself to the priest.
\v 20 The priest will examine it, and if it is indeed more than \add just\add* skin deep and its hair has turned white, then the priest must pronounce that person as ‘unclean’ because an infectious skin disease has broken out from the \add original\add* boil.
\v 21 However, if the priest’s examination finds not white hair on it, and it’s not more than skin deep, and it’s fading, then he will \add order\add* that person quarantined for seven days,
\v 22 and if it spreads further on their skin, then the priest must declare that person as ‘unclean’ because it’s infectious.
\v 23 But if the bright spot hasn’t spread \add during the quarantine\add*, then it’s \add just\add* the scar from the boil and the priest can pronounce the person as ‘clean’.
\p
\v 24 If someone gets a burn on their skin, and the burn area becomes reddish-white or a white spot,
\v 25 then the priest must examine it, and if the hair on the spot has turned white and the infection looks more than skin deep, then a skin disease has broken out in the burn, and the priest must pronounce that person as ‘unclean’ because it’s infectious.
\v 26 However, if the priest sees that there’s no white hair in the spot, and it’s not more than skin deep, and it’s fading, then he will \add order\add* that person quarantined for seven days.
\v 27 On the seventh day, the priest will re-examine the person, and if it’s spread further, he must pronounce that person as ‘unclean’ because it’s an infectious skin disease.
\v 28 But if the bright spot hasn’t spread on the skin, and it faded \add more\add*, then it was \add just\add* swelling from the burn. Then the priest will pronounce that person to be ‘clean’ because it was \add just\add* the scar caused by their burn.
\p
\v 29 If a man or woman has an infection in their scalp or under their beard,
\v 30 then the priest should inspect the infection. If it appears to be more than skin deep, and there’s any thin yellow hairs in it, then the priest must pronounce that person as ‘unclean’ \add because\add* it’s an itchy and infectious skin infection on the scalp or face.
\v 31 However, if the priest notices an itchy infection yet it’s only skin deep and there’s no black hair on it, then he should quarantine that person for seven days.
\v 32 On the seventh day, the priest will re-examine that person and if the spot hasn’t widened and \add still\add* only appears to be skin deep,
\v 33 then that person should shave \add their head\add*, and then the priest should quarantine them for a further seven days.
\v 34 Then \add again\add* on the seventh day, the priest will inspect it, and if the spot hasn’t widened after being shaved and it doesn’t seem to be more than skin deep, then the priest can pronounce that person as ‘clean’ once they’ve washed their clothes.
\v 35 However, if it did spread on their skin after that,
\v 36 then the priest must inspect it \add again\add*, and if it’s spread then that person is ‘unclean’ (whether or not there’s any yellow hair).
\v 37 But if it seems that the spot hasn’t spread and dark hair is growing in it again, then that person’s not infectious and the priest can pronounce them as ‘clean’.
\p
\v 38 If a man or woman finds very white spots on their skin,
\v 39 then the priest must examine them. If the spots on their skin are dull white, then it’s a skin rash that’s broken out and they are ‘clean’.
\p
\v 40 If a man’s head has become bare, then he’s \add gone\add* bald and is \add still\add* ‘clean’.
\v 41 Similarly, if the hair is receding from the front corners, he’s going bald and is \add still\add* ‘clean’.
\v 42 However, if there’s a balding spot with a reddish-white infection, it’s a skin disease breaking out via that spot
\v 43 and the priest must examine him. If it’s a balding spot with a reddish-white infection that looks like a \add contagious\add* skin disease,
\v 44 then he’s contagious and ‘unclean’. The priest must pronounce his as ‘unclean’ because of that infection on his head.
\p
\v 45 Anyone who’s developed an infectious skin disease must tear their clothes and leave their hair loose, and must cover their mouth and call out, ‘Unclean, unclean.’
\v 46 They will remain ‘unclean’ as long as they \add still\add* have that infection, and must live alone outside the camp.
\s1 Regulations about moulds and fungi
\p
\v 47 When a \add piece of\add* clothing has a mould growing on it, whether it’s made of wool or linen,
\v 48 or whether it’s something woven from wool or linen, or something made from leather,
\v 49 if the growth is greenish or reddish in colour, then it’s something that can spread and it must be shown to the priest.
\v 50 The priest must examine the growth, and quarantine the article for seven days.
\v 51 On the seventh day, if the growth has spread on that piece of material or leather equipment, and it’s confirmed as spreadable and is ‘unclean’.
\v 52 The owner must burn the piece of clothing or equipment that contains the infection because it’s contagious, so it must be completely burnt up.
\p
\v 53 However if the priest notices that the growth hasn’t spread on the clothing or on the woven or leather material,
\v 54 then the priest must order that the material be washed and then quarantined for a further seven days.
\v 55 \add On the seventh day,\add* the priest must inspect that washed material, and if the growth hasn’t changed its appearance, even if it hasn’t spread, then it’s ‘unclean’ and must be burnt in the fire—it’s a mildew that’s on the front or back of the material.
\v 56 However, if the priest notices that the growth has faded after being washed, then it can be torn out of the clothing or piece of leather or woven material.
\v 57 If it \add then\add* reappears on that clothing or material, then it’s breaking out, so then that article must be burnt in the fire.
\v 58 On the other hand, if the growth vanished after being washed, then it must be washed a second time and then it will be ‘clean’.
\p
\v 59 The above are the regulations concerning any growth on a piece of woollen or linen clothing or fabric, or any leather article, to pronounce it ‘clean’ or ‘unclean’.
\c 14
\s1 Purification after a skin disease
\p
\v 1 Then Yahweh told Moses:
\p
\v 2 These are the regulations for the purification of a person who had a skin disease. They must be taken to a priest
\v 3 who’ll go outside the camp \add to meet them\add*. The priest must inspect \add that person\add*, and if the skin disease has indeed left them,
\v 4 then the priest will order them to bring him two live, ‘clean’ birds, some cedar \add wood\add*, some scarlet \add thread\add*, and some hyssop \add twigs\add*.
\v 5 The priest must tell the person to slaughter one bird so the blood runs into a clay jug containing water from a spring or creek.
\v 6 Then the priest will take the live bird, along with the cedar wood, the scarlet \add thread\add*, and the hyssop \add twigs\add*, and dip them all into that blood of the slaughtered bird (mixed with the fresh water),
\v 7 and flick it seven times onto the person who’s being purified from the skin disease. Then the priest can pronounce them as ‘clean’ \add enough to re-enter the community\add*, and release the live bird \add to fly\add* into the countryside.
\v 8 The person who’s being purified must then wash their clothes, shave all their hair, and then bathe in \add flowing\add* water. After that, they can re-enter the camp but must live outside their tent for seven \add more\add* days.
\v 9 On the seventh day, that person must shave off all their hair (including eyebrows and beard \add if appropriate\add*), wash their clothes and bathe themself in \add flowing\add* water, then they’ll be ‘clean’.
\p
\v 10 Then on the eighth day, that person must get two one-year-old \add male\add* lambs, and one female lamb, \add all\add* with no defects, along with three kilograms of fine flour mixed with oil as a grain offering, as well as half a litre of \add olive\add* oil.
\v 11 \add That must be taken to\add* the priest who’d pronounced them as ‘clean’ will accompany them and all those offerings to stand in front of Yahweh at the entrance to the sacred tent.
\v 12 Then the priest must take one of the male lambs and the container of oil and present it as a guilt offering, then he’ll raise them as a wave offering in front of Yahweh.
\v 13 Then that person must slaughter that lamb in the sacred place where sin offerings and burnt offerings are slaughtered, because this guilt offering is like a sin offering in that it’s for the priest—it’s very sacred.
\v 14 Then the priest must take some of the blood from that guilt offering, and put it on the lobe of the right ear of the person being purified, and on their right thumb and right big toe.
\v 15 Then the priest must take the container of \add olive\add* oil and pour some into his left hand.
\v 16 Then he’ll dip his right finger into that oil on his left palm, and with that \add same\add* finger, flick it seven times towards Yahweh.
\v 17 Then the priest must put some of the remaining oil on his palm onto the lobe of the right ear of the person being purified, and on their right thumb and right big toe—on top of the blood from the guilt offering.
\v 18 Then the priest will put \add all\add* the remaining oil from his palm onto the head of the person being purified, and the priest will then make that person right with Yahweh.
\p
\v 19 Then the priest must make the sin offering and make that person right after being purified from their ‘uncleanness’. After that that person will slaughter \add lamb for\add* the burnt offering.
\v 20 Then the priest must offer that burnt offering and the grain offering on the fire on the altar and he will make that person right again and he will be ‘clean’.
\p
\v 21 However, if that person is poor and cannot afford \add the multiple lambs\add*, then they must take \em one\em* lamb to wave as the guilt offering and to make them right again, along with \+em one\+em* kilogram of fine flour mixed with oil for the grain offering, and the half litre of oil,
\v 22 plus two doves or young pigeons—whichever they can afford—one for a sin offering and the other for the burnt offering.
\v 23 Then on that eighth day, he’ll take them to the priest at the entrance of the sacred tent for purification in front of Yahweh.
\v 24 Then the priest should take the guilt offering lamb and the container of oil and lift them up as a wave offering in front of Yahweh.
\v 25 Then the person should slaughter that guilt offering lamb, and the priest must take some of that blood and put it on the lobe of the right ear of the person being purified, and on their right thumb and right big toe.
\v 26 Then he must pour some of the oil onto his left palm
\v 27 and with his right finger he must flick it seven times towards Yahweh.
\v 28 Then the priest must put some of the oil from his palm onto the lobe of the right ear of the person being purified, and on their right thumb and right big toe—on top of the blood from the guilt offering.
\v 29 Then the priest will put \add all\add* the remaining oil from his palm onto the head of the person being purified, and the priest will then make that person right with Yahweh.
\v 30 Then he must offer one of the birds (dove or pigeon—whatever that person could afford)
\v 31 as a sin offering and the other as a burnt offering along with the grain offering and he will make that person right again with Yahweh.
\v 32 \add The above\add* are the regulations for anyone who has a skin disease but who couldn’t afford the regular purification offerings.
\s1 Treating contaminated homes
\p
\v 33 Then Yahweh told Moses and Aaron:
\p
\v 34 “When you enter into Canaan which I’m giving you all as a possession, and if I cause an infection in one of your homes in that land,
\v 35 then the homeowner must go and inform the priest that something like an infection has appeared in their house.
\v 36 The priest must order that everything be removed out of the house before he comes to inspect it so that it won’t all be pronounced as ‘unclean’. After that’s done, he’ll go to see the house
\v 37 and examine the growth. If the growth is greenish or reddish and seems more than surface deep,
\v 38 then the priest must leave the house via the \add main\add* entrance and quarantine the house for seven days.
\v 39 On the seventh day, the priest must return and examine it \add again\add*, and if the growth has spread on the house walls,
\v 40 then he must order that those infected stones be pulled out of the wall and thrown outside the city into a place \add that’s considered\add* ‘unclean’.
\v 41 Then \add the homeowner\add* must scrape all the plaster off the inside walls and \add also\add* dispose of that at that ‘unclean’ place.
\v 42 Then they can replace the stones that were removed, and apply fresh plaster to the house.
\p
\v 43 However, if the growth returns and breaks out \add again\add* in the house \add even\add* after the stones were replaced and the walls were replastered,
\v 44 then the priest must come \add back\add* and reexamine it. If the growth has spread on the walls, then it’s a malignant growth and the house is ‘unclean’.
\v 45 The owner must tear the house down—its stones and timber and all the mortar, must be carted outside the city into a place \add that’s considered\add* ‘unclean’.
\v 46 Any person that enters that house after the priest had quarantined it, will be ‘unclean’ until that evening.
\v 47 Any person who lies down or eats in that house, must wash their clothes.
\p
\v 48 However, if the priest came back after the house was replastered and after his inspection, find that the growth hasn’t reappeared, then he can pronounce the house as ‘clean’ because the growth has gone.
\v 49 Then he must take two birds, cedar wood, scarlet thread, and some hyssop \add twigs\add*,
\v 50 and slaughter one bird so the blood runs into a clay jug containing water from a spring or creek.
\v 51 Then the priest must take the live bird, along with the cedar wood, the scarlet \add thread\add*, the hyssop \add twigs\add* and the live bird, and dip them all into that blood of the slaughtered bird (mixed with the fresh water), and flick it seven times onto the house.
\v 52 \add In that way,\add* he’ll purify the house with the bird’s blood and the fresh water, and with the live bird and the cedar \add wood\add*, the hyssop \add twigs\add* and the scarlet \add thread\add*.
\v 53 Then he must release the live bird \add to fly\add* into the countryside, thus making the house right with God, and then it’ll be ‘clean’.
\p
\v 54 The above are the regulations concerning any serious skin diseases and itchiness,
\v 55 as well as growths on clothing and buildings
\v 56 and swellings, rashes, and bright spots \add on the skin\add*,
\v 57 so that you’ll know how to determine what’s ‘unclean’ or ‘clean’. Those are the regulations for infectious growths.
\c 15
\s1 Dealing with bodily discharges
\p
\v 1 Then Yahweh told Moses and Aaron:
\p
\v 2 Tell the Israelites that whenever any man has a discharge from his genitals, then he becomes ‘unclean’.
\v 3 He becomes ‘unclean’ whether his body secretes the flow or if it’s blocked—either way he’s ‘unclean’.
\v 4 Any bed that he was lying on during \add or after\add* the discharge becomes ‘unclean’, as does anything he sits on.
\v 5 Anyone who touches that bed must wash their clothes and bathe themself in water, and that person will be ‘unclean’ until the evening.
\v 6 Similarly, anyone who sits on the place where he sat must wash their clothes and bathe themself in water, and that person will be ‘unclean’ until the evening.
\v 7 Anyone who touches the man who had a discharge must wash their clothes and bathe themself in water, and that person will be ‘unclean’ until the evening.
\v 8 If that man who had a discharge spits on some person who was ‘clean’, then that person must wash their clothes and bathe themself in water, and they’ll be ‘unclean’ until the evening.
\v 9 Any thing that the man who had a discharge rides on, will become ‘unclean’,
\v 10 and anyone who touches it will be ‘unclean’ until the evening, plus anyone who carries any of those things, must wash their clothes and bathe themself in water, and they’ll be ‘unclean’ until the evening.
\v 11 Anyone the man who had a discharge touches and do didn’t rinse their hands in water, must wash their clothes and bathe themself in water, and they’ll be ‘unclean’ until the evening.
\v 12 Any container that the man who had a discharge touches, must be smashed if it’s made from clay and rinsed with water if it’s made from wood.
\p
\v 13 When the man’s discharge ceases, he must mark off seven days for his purification, and then he must wash his clothes and bathe himself in water, and \add after that,\add* he’ll be ‘clean’.
\v 14 Then on the eighth day, he must take two doves or young pigeons, and stand in front of Yahweh at the entrance to the sacred tent. There he should give them to a priest,
\v 15 and the priest must offer one as a sin offering and the other as a burnt offering, thus making the man right with Yahweh for his discharge.
\p
\v 16 Now when a man has an emission of semen, then he must bathe his entire body in water, and he’ll be ‘unclean’ until the evening.
\v 17 He must also wash all his clothing, and any leather articles on him that came into contact with the semen and all of that will be ‘unclean’ until the evening.
\v 18 When a man and a woman ‘sleep together’, then they must both bathe in water, and they’ll \add both\add* be ‘unclean’ until the evening.\f + \fr 15:18 \ft Note that if they had sexual relations in the evening, say 9pm, then they’d be ‘unclean’ all the next day and until the \+em following\+em* evening (because the Jewish ‘day’ begins after dusk—rather than at midnight like modern Western society counts time).\f*
\p
\v 19 When a woman has her menstrual period, she’ll be ‘unclean’ for seven days, and anyone who touches her during that time will be ‘unclean’ until the evening.
\v 20 Anything that she lies on or sits on during that time will become ‘unclean’.
\v 21 Anyone who touches her bed must wash their clothes and bathe in water, and they’ll be ‘unclean’ until the evening.
\v 22 Anyone who touches anything that she sat on must wash their clothes and bathe in water, and they’ll be ‘unclean’ until the evening,
\v 23 i.e., whether it’s her bed or something she sat on, anyone who touches it will be ‘unclean’ until the evening.
\v 24 If a man sleeps with a menstruating woman and gets blood on him, then he’ll be ‘unclean’ for seven days, and any bed that he lies on will \add also\add* become ‘unclean’.
\p
\v 25 If any woman has a blood flow when it’s not her period, or keeps bleeding beyond her menstrual period, all those days will be regarded the same as her menstruation so she’ll be ‘unclean’.
\v 26 Any bed that she lies on as long as she’s discharging blood will be regarded the same as during her menstruation and will be ‘unclean’, as will anything she sits on.
\v 27 Also, any person who touches them will be ‘unclean’ and they must wash their clothes and bathe in water, and they’ll be ‘unclean’ until the evening.
\v 28 Once the flow stops, then the woman must count off seven days, and then she’ll be ‘clean’ after that.
\v 29 Then on the eighth day, she must take two doves or young pigeons and give them to a priest at the entrance to the sacred tent,
\v 30 and he will offer one as a sin offering and the other as a burnt offering, thus making her right with Yahweh from her ‘unclean’ flow.
\p
\v 31 You two must keep the Israelite people separate from their ‘uncleanness’, then they won’t defile my residence that’s among them, and cause their \add own\add* death.
\p
\v 32 The above are the regulations for any man with a discharge or a seminal emission that makes him ‘unclean’,
\v 33 plus any woman who is menstruating or has a bodily discharge, as well as any man who sleeps with an ‘unclean’ woman.
\c 16
\s1 The day for making things right
\p
\v 1 Yahweh spoke to Moses after the death of two of Aaron’s sons when they approached Yahweh \add wrongly\add* and died,
\v 2 and he told Moses:
\p Tell your brother Aaron that if he doesn’t \add want to\add* die, then he mustn’t \add just\add* enter the holiest room inside the curtain at any old time. The atonement lid is on the \add sacred\add* box, and I will appear in a cloud over that lid.
\p
\v 3 This is \add how\add* Aaron must enter the sacred tent: \add he must acquire\add* a young bull for a sin offering, and a ram for a burnt offering.
\v 4 He must bathe himself in water and then put on linen underclothes, followed by his sacred linen tunic and tie the linen sash around it. Then he must wrap the linen turban on. Those are \add all\add* sacred clothes.
\p
\v 5 Then Aaron must \add acquire\add* two young goats for a sin offering and one ram for a burnt offering.
\v 6 He must present the bull as a sin offering for himself to make both him and his household right with God.
\v 7 Then he must take the two goats and make them stand in front of Yahweh at the entrance to the sacred tent,
\v 8 and throw dice to decide which one is for Yahweh, and one is to be the ‘scapegoat’.
\v 9 Then Aaron must present the goat that’s for Yahweh, and offer it as a sin offering,
\v 10 but the chosen ‘scapegoat’ must be kept standing there in front of Yahweh, because it’ll be used to make the people right with Yahweh, \add and be sent\add* into the wilderness.
\p
\v 11 Then Aaron must present the bull for his own sin offering to make him and his household right with God, then he’ll slaughter the bull for the sin offering for himself.
\v 12 Then he must get a panful of burning coals off the altar in front of Yahweh as well as two handfuls of finely-ground, fragrant incense, and take them inside the curtain
\v 13 where he’ll put the incense on the coals in Yahweh’s presence. Then the \add smoke\add* cloud from the incense will cover the atonement lid on the \add sacred\add* chest, and he won’t die.
\v 14 Then he must take some of the bull’s blood, and using his finger, he must flick the blood seven times onto the eastern the side of the lid and seven times onto the front of the lid.
\p
\v 15 Then Aaron must slaughter the goat for the people’s sin offering, and take its blood inside the curtain, and do the same with it that he did to the bull’s blood—flicking it onto the side and front of the atonement lid,
\v 16 thus making the Israelite people right with Yahweh for their uncleanness and their rebellion and disobedience. That’s also for the sacred tent which is among the people with their uncleanness.
\v 17 No one else can enter the sacred tent when Aaron enters to make atonement in the sacred room until he leaves \add again\add*. He must make atonement for himself and his household, as well as for the whole Israelite community,
\v 18 then he’ll go out to the altar that’s in front of Yahweh and make atonement for that—he must take some of the bull’s blood and some of the goat’s blood and put it on the \add four\add* projecting corners of the altar.
\v 19 Then he must flick blood on to it seven times with his finger, thus purifying it and removing the uncleanness of the Israelite people from it.
\s1 The goat taking the blame
\p
\v 20 When Aaron has finished purifying the sacred room, the sacred tent, and the altar, then he should present the live goat.
\v 21 He must place both his hands on the goat’s head, and confess all of Israel’s disobedience, rebellion, and shortcomings—\add symbolically\add* placing them onto the goat’s head, and then he must choose a man that’s nearby to take it into the wilderness.
\v 22 Then he’ll release the goat there in the wilderness, and the goat will carry all their sins into that barren land.
\p
\v 23 Then Aaron must enter the sacred tent and take off the linen garment that he’d put on as he’d entered into the sacred room and leave them there.
\v 24 Then he must wash himself in water in a sacred place, put on his \add regular\add* clothes, and go out and make his burnt offering and the burnt offering for the people, thus making both himself and the people right with Yahweh.
\v 25 Then he must burn the fat from the sin offering up on the altar.
\v 26 Also, the person who led away the ‘scapegoat’ must wash his clothes and bathe himself in water, and then he can reenter the camp.
\v 27 Then \add the remains of\add* the bull and the goat from the sin offerings (whose blood was taken into the sacred room to make atonement) must be taken to a place outside the camp where they must be burnt—skins, flesh, and dung.
\v 28 The man who burnt them must then wash his clothes and bathe his body in water, and then he can reenter the camp.
\s1 The national purification day
\p
\v 29 This will be a permanent regulation for you all:
\p On the 10th of the seventh month \add (of Tishrei—around September/October)\add*, all of you including those who are native-born as well as foreigners living among you, must humble yourselves and not work that day
\v 30 because on that day, the high priest will purify you all from your sins to make you right with Yahweh, and you’ll be ‘clean’.
\v 31 It’ll be a very sacred rest day for you, and you all must humble yourselves—a permanent regulation \add for you all\add*.
\v 32 The priest who’s anointed and ordained to succeed his father \add as high priest\add* must be the one to make atonement, and he must be wearing his sacred linen clothes.
\v 33 He will \add first\add* purify the sacred room and the sacred tent, and the altar, and then make all the priests right with Yahweh and \add finally,\add* the entire community.
\v 34 That will become a permanent regulation every year to make all the Israelites right with Yahweh because of their sins.
\p So Moses did exactly as Yahweh had instructed him.
\c 17
\s1 Slaughtering animals
\p
\v 1 Then Yahweh told Moses:
\p
\v 2 Tell Aaron and his sons and all the Israelites that these are the instructions from Yahweh:
\v 3 No Israelite is allowed to slaughter a bull or lamb or goat \add either\add* inside the camp or outside it.
\v 4 If they’re not taken to the entrance to the sacred tent to be presented as an offering to Yahweh in front of his residence, then that person will be held guilty of its blood—they’ve shed blood so they must be cut off from his people.
\v 5 The Israelites must stop sacrificing their animals in the countryside, and \add instead\add* bring them to Yahweh at the entrance to the sacred tent, \add i.e., they must\add* bring them to the priest and sacrifice them as peace offerings to Yahweh.
\v 6 Then the priest will splash the blood onto Yahweh’s altar at the tent entrance, and burn up the fat to become a soothing aroma for Yahweh.
\v 7 Then they won’t be able to continue offering their sacrifices to the goat demons that they’re prostituting themselves to. The above will be a permanent regulation throughout the generations.
\p
\v 8 You must tell them that if an Israelite or a foreigner living among them, makes a burnt offering or sacrifice
\v 9 without bringing it to Yahweh at the sacred tent entrance, then that person must be cut off from the community.
\s1 The law concerning the blood
\p
\v 10 \add If there’s\add* any Israelite or foreigner living among them, who eats blood, then I’ll oppose that person and remove them from the community
\v 11 because the body’s life is in the blood. I myself have given you blood to make atonement for your lives at the altar, because it’s the blood that can atone for life.
\v 12 That’s why I told the Israelites that none of them must eat blood—\add not even\add* the foreigners that live among them may eat blood.
\p
\v 13 So any person (Israelite or foreigner living among them) who catches wild game (including birds) to eat, they must pour its blood out and cover it with soil
\v 14 because the life of their bodies is the blood that’s in them. Again, \add that’s why\add* I’ve told the Israelites not to eat the blood of any living thing because the life is in the blood, and anyone who eats it must be cut off from the community.
\p
\v 15 Anyone who eats \add part of\add* a carcass \add they found\add* or one that was killed by wild animals, must wash their clothes and bathe in water, and they’ll be ‘unclean’ until the evening, and then they’ll be ‘clean’ \add again\add*.
\v 16 However, if that person doesn’t wash \add their clothes\add* or bathe, then they must bear \add the punishment for\add* their own disobedience.
\c 18
\s1 Sexual relationships
\p
\v 1 Then Yahweh told Moses:
\p
\v 2 Tell the Israelite that I am their god Yahweh.
\v 3 They mustn’t behave as if they’re still there in Egypt, nor should they behave like the Canaanites that were living in the land that I’m taking you all into, and don’t emulate their customs and practices.
\v 4 You all must imbibe my values and follow my instructions. I am your god Yahweh.
\v 5 You all must follow my instructions and my values—those who do so will live. I am your god Yahweh.
\p
\v 6 None of you may ever flirt with any close relative to try to get them to undress. I am Yahweh.
\v 7 You must never mess with your father’s naked body, or your mother’s—no, she’s your mother so don’t ever take advantage of her nakedness.
\v 8 Don’t ever mess with any of your father’s wives, because their naked bodies are the realm of your father.
\v 9 Don’t flirt with your sister’s nakedness, whether she’s a full sister, a half sister, or an adopted sister.
\v 10 Don’t flirt with your grandchildren’s nakedness, because you’d be absolutely shaming yourself.
\v 11 Don’t flirt with your step-sister’s nakedness, because she’s your sister and her nakedness isn’t yours to enjoy.
\v 12 Don’t flirt with your aunts nakedness on your father’s side, because she’s closely related to your father.
\v 13 Don’t flirt with your aunt’s nakedness on your mother’s side, because she’s closely related to your mother.
\v 14 Don’t flirt with your uncle’s nakedness on your father’s side, and keep away from his wife, because she’s your aunt.
\v 15 Don’t flirt with your daughter-in-law’s nakedness, because she’s your son’s wife and her nakedness isn’t yours to enjoy.
\v 16 Don’t flirt with your sister-in-law, because her naked body is the realm of your brother.
\v 17 Don’t flirt with the nakedness of \add both\add* a woman and her daughter or her granddaughter, because they’re related to each other—that would be perverse.
\v 18 Don’t take your wife’s sister to be a junior wife while your wife is still alive.
\p
\v 19 Don’t approach a woman who’s menstruating, to undress her, because she’s ‘unclean’.
\v 20 Don’t sleep with someone else’s wife—your child with her would be ‘unclean’.
\v 21 Don’t sacrifice your children to Molech so you don’t profane your god’s reputation. I am Yahweh.
\v 22 Don’t have sexual relations with a man like with a woman—it’s an abomination.
\v 23 \add Men,\add* don’t have sexual relations with any animal as that would make you ‘unclean’. And women, don’t give yourself sexually to any animal as that’s perverse.
\p
\v 24 Don’t make yourselves ‘unclean’ in any of the above ways, because all of those things were done by the people groups that I’m driving out of this land ahead of you all.
\v 25 The land had become ‘unclean’ so I made them pay for their wickedness and \add so\add* the land will vomit out its inhabitants.
\v 26 However, you all must obey my instructions and decisions and not tolerate any of that perverse behaviour—not you who are native-born, nor any foreigner living among you all,
\v 27 because all that perversity was done by those living there before you all, and the land became ‘unclean’.
\v 28 \add Don’t think\add* that the land won’t vomit you all out just like it’s doing to the people groups that have been \add there\add* before you,
\v 29 because everyone who does any of those perversities will be cut off from their community.
\v 30 So you all must carefully guard against practising any of those detestable customs which were previously done \add there\add* and which would make you all ‘unclean’. I am your god Yahweh.
\c 19
\s1 Various moral imperatives
\p
\v 1 Then Yahweh told Moses:
\p
\v 2 Tell the entire Israelite community that they must be separate from others, because I, your god Yahweh, am holy \add (i.e., separated from impurity)\add*.
\v 3 Everyone must respect and obey their parents, and observe my rest days. I am your god Yahweh.
\p
\v 4 Don’t turn to worthless idols, and don’t make cast metal gods for yourselves. I am your god Yahweh.
\p
\v 5 When you all sacrifice peace offerings, do it correctly so that you’ll all be accepted.
\v 6 \add The meat from\add* your sacrifice must be eaten that same day or the next day—anything left until the third day must be thrown into the fire.
\v 7 If it was to be eaten on the third day, it would be ‘unclean’ and not acceptable,
\v 8 and the people eating it would have to bear their own guilt for profaning Yahweh’s holiness, and that person must be cut off from the community.
\p
\v 9 When you all harvest your crops off your land, you mustn’t cut the grain right to the very edge of your field, and nor can you all pick up any dropped stalks.
\v 10 In your vineyards, you must not pick every last grape or pick up any that fall on the ground, because they must be left for the poor and for the foreigners. I am your god Yahweh.
\p
\v 11 You all mustn’t steal or deceive, or defraud any fellow citizen,
\v 12 and don’t use my name to seal a promise and then \add go ahead and\add* ruin my reputation. I am Yahweh.
\p
\v 13 Don’t oppress your neighbours or steal from them. Don’t fail to pay a day-labourer as they finish each day’s work.
\v 14 Don’t curse a deaf person, or put something in front of a blind person \add to trip them\add*. You must respect and obey your god. I am Yahweh.
\p
\v 15 Don’t judge unfairly—showing partiality to the poor, or honouring those who are well-known—you must \add always\add* judge your fellow citizens fairly.
\v 16 Don’t go around slandering people. Don’t take advantage of your neighbour’s death. I am Yahweh.
\p
\v 17 You mustn’t hate your brother. Certainly rebuke those you interact with so you don’t get blamed for their bad behaviour.
\v 18 Don’t try to get revenge against others or hold a grudge against any of your people, but \add instead,\add* love your neighbour as yourself. I am Yahweh.
\p
\v 19 Follow my instructions—don’t crossbreed two different kinds of animals, don’t sow two different crops in your field, and don’t wear clothing made of different kinds of thread.
\p
\v 20 If a man impregnates a woman who’s destined to be someone else’s slave and she hasn’t been freed, compensation needs to be \add paid\add*.\f + \fr 19:20 \ft We as modern readers don’t understand all the dynamics of this seemingly complex social situation.\f* They mustn’t be executed, because she hadn’t been freed.
\v 21 However, that man must take a ram to Yahweh at the sacred tent’s entrance for a guilt offering,
\v 22 and the priest will make him right with Yahweh by means of that guilt offering ram, and then he’ll be forgiven for his transgression.
\p
\v 23 When you all enter into the land and plant various kinds of fruit trees, you mustn’t eat the fruit for the first three years—it should be \add regarded as\add* ‘uncircumcised’ \add for that duration\add*.
\v 24 Then in the fourth year, all that fruit must be \add regarded as\add* ‘sacred’—praise offerings for Yahweh.
\v 25 Finally, in the fifth year, you can eat that fruit and add that produce to your harvest. I am your god Yahweh.
\p
\v 26 Don’t eat anything with the blood \add still in it\add*. Don’t practice divination or witchcraft.
\v 27 Don’t \add have haircuts that\add* round off your hair at the sides, and \add men\add*, don’t trim the edges of your beards.
\v 28 Don’t make cuts on your bodies when someone dies, and don’t tattoo yourselves. I am Yahweh.
\p
\v 29 don’t defile your daughter by making her prostitute herself, so the country doesn’t prostitute itself and become full of wickedness.
\v 30 Keep my rest days, and respect my sanctuary. I am Yahweh.
\p
\v 31 Don’t turn to mediums and diviners—don’t make yourselves ‘unclean’ by means of them. I am your god Yahweh.
\p
\v 32 Stand up for those with grey hair, and honour the presence of the elderly. Honour and respect your god. I am Yahweh.
\p
\v 33 Don’t take advantage of foreigners when they stay in your land—
\v 34 you all must treat foreigners the same as native-born Israelites and love them like you love yourselves, because you were all foreigners in Egypt. I am your god Yahweh.
\p
\v 35 Don’t allow injustice \add either\add* in judging cases, \add or by trading with rigged\add* weights and measures—
\v 36 your scales and weights and measuring containers must be honest. I am your god Yahweh who brought you all out of Egypt.
\v 37 You all must obey my instructions and decisions and stick to them. I am Yahweh.
\c 20
\s1 Penalties for disobedience
\p
\v 1 Then Yahweh told Moses:
\p
\v 2 Tell the Israelite people that if any of them, or any foreigner staying in Israel, gives any of their children to Molech, then that person must definitely be executed—the people must do it by throwing rocks at them.
\v 3 \add Yes,\add* I myself will personally punish that person and cut them off from the people for giving their child to Molech and so making my sanctuary ‘unclean’ and profaning my sacred name.
\v 4 If the people of the land \add just\add* hides their eyes from \add seeing\add* anyone offering their child to Molech so they don’t have to execute that person,
\v 5 then I’ll \add also\add* remove that \add other\add* person and their clan. I’ll cut them off from their people, along with anyone else who practises prostitution by prostituting themselves to Molech.
\p
\v 6 I’ll take action against any person who turns to mediums or diviners to prostitute themselves to them, and I’ll cut them off from their people.
\v 7 You must keep yourselves separate from those kinds of things to be holy, because I, Yahweh, am your god.
\v 8 You all must follow my instructions and implement them. I am Yahweh who makes you different from other people groups.
\p
\v 9 Any person who curses their father or mother must be executed—their blood will be on their own head for cursing their parents.
\p
\v 10 Any man who commits adultery with another man’s wife, maybe even his neighbours wife, must be executed—both the man and the woman.
\v 11 Any man who sleeps with any of his father’s wives, has exposed his father’s nakedness. Both of them must be executed—their blood is on their own heads.
\v 12 If a man sleeps with his daughter-in-law, both of them must be executed. That’s perversion and so their blood is on their own heads.
\v 13 If a man has a sexual relationship with another man, both of them have done something detestable and both of them must be executed—their blood is on their own heads.
\v 14 If a man takes both a mother and her daughter as wives, then that’s wicked. They must all be burnt to death so that depravity won’t \add take root\add* among you.
\p
\v 15 If a man \add tries to\add* have sex with an animal, that man must be executed and the animal must \add also\add* be killed.
\v 16 If a woman presents herself sexually to any animal, you must execute the woman and kill the animal—their blood is on their own heads.
\p
\v 17 If a man sleeps with his sister (whether on his father’s or mother’s side) and they see each other’s nakedness, it’s a disgrace and they must be cut off from their people—he’s exposed her nakedness and he must bear his wickedness.
\v 18 If a man sleeps with a woman who’s menstruating, he’s exposed her nakedness and uncovered the source of her blood, \add so\add* they must both be cut off from among their people.
\p
\v 19 You mustn’t expose the nakedness of your sister (whether on your father’s side or mother’s side) because \add that’s like\add* exposing your own body. They must bear their wickedness.
\v 20 A man who sleeps with his aunt, he’s exposed his uncle’s nakedness. They’ll bear their wickedness \add by\add* dying childless.
\v 21 If a man marries his brother’s wife, it’s indecent. He’s exposed his nakedness \add and\add* they’ll be childless.
\p
\v 22 You all must obey my instructions and decisions and stick to them, and then the land that I’m about to take you into, won’t vomit you all out.
\v 23 Don’t follow the customs of the peoples that I’ll drive out ahead of you all, because they do all of the above things, and I detest them
\v 24 and I’ve told you all that you yourselves will inherit their land—I’ll give it to you to take possession of. It’s a land that’s very fertile. I am your god Yahweh who separates you all from the \add other\add* people \add groups\add*.
\v 25 You all must distinguish between ‘clean’ and ‘unclean’ animals and birds. Don’t make yourselves detestable \add to me\add* with any animal or bird or crawling creature which I’ve distinguished for you all as ‘unclean’.
\v 26 You all must be ‘holy’ to me, because I Yahweh am holy, and I’ve set you all apart from the other people groups to be mine.
\p
\v 27 Any man or woman who’s a medium or diviner must be executed—the people must do it by throwing rocks at them.
\c 21
\s1 Stricter instructions for priests
\p
\v 1 Then Yahweh told Moses:
\p Tell Aaron’s descendants the priests that they must not defile themselves among the Israelites, by touching anything that’s not living
\v 2 apart from the body of a close relative like a parent, child, brother
\v 3 or unmarried sister—the priest can become ‘unclean’ by means of one of them.
\v 4 He must not make himself ‘unclean’ \add if it’s the death of\add* a relative only by marriage.
\v 5 They must not shave their heads or trim the edges of their beards or cut their bodies \add in grief\add*.
\p
\v 6 The priests must stay separate for their God and they must not tarnish his reputation because they present the gifts to Yahweh—their god’s food
\v 7 They mustn’t marry a woman who was a prostitute or who has defiled herself \add some other way\add* or who is divorced from her husband, because they’re separated for their god.
\v 8 You must treat him as set apart because he’s the one presenting your god’s food. He must be ‘holy’ for you, because I, Yahweh, am holy and I set you all apart as ‘holy’.
\v 9 If a priest’s daughter prostituted herself, she would be profaning her father \add so\add* must be burnt \add to death\add*.
\p
\v 10 The priest who is chief of all his brothers and who had the anointing oil poured over his head, and who has the responsibility of wearing the \add special\add* clothing, must not wear his hair loose, and he must not tear his clothes \add in grief\add*.
\v 11 He mustn’t approach \em any\em* dead body—not \add even\add* his \add own\add* parents—he mustn’t make himself ‘unclean’.
\v 12 \add Even for a death,\add* he mustn’t leave \add his duties in\add* the sanctuary, and he mustn’t profane the sanctuary of his god, because he’s been consecrated with the anointing oil. I am Yahweh.
\v 13 He can \add only\add* marry a virgin—
\v 14 he mustn’t take a widow or a divorced woman, or a defiled prostitute. He can only marry an unmarried, Israelite girl,
\v 15 then he won’t profane his descendants among his relatives, because I am Yahweh who sets him apart as ‘holy’.
\p
\v 16 Then Yahweh told Moses,
\p
\v 17 Tell Aaron that none of his descendants who have a deformity should ever approach to present the food for his god.
\v 18 Yes, no man who has a deformity must approach \add to offer a sacrifice\add*, i.e., no one who’s blind, lame, mutilated, or with a swelling,
\v 19 or with a broken foot or hand,
\v 20 or a hunchback or dwarf, or with an eye defect, with a scab or rash, or anyone with a crushed testicle.
\v 21 Any descendant of Aaron the priest with a blemish mustn’t approach to preset a gift to Yahweh—no that person mustn’t approach to present god’s food.
\v 22 However, they can eat those gifts for God, including the sacred and very sacred portions.
\v 23 Despite that, they mustn’t enter past the curtain or approach the altar if they have a defect on their body. They mustn’t profane my sanctuary because I am Yahweh who sets them apart as ‘holy’.
\p
\v 24 So Moses told \add all\add* that to Aaron and his sons, and all the Israelites.
\c 22
\s1 Offerings to Yahweh become sacred
\p
\v 1 Then Yahweh told Moses:
\p
\v 2 Tell Aaron and his sons that they must carefully honour the holy things \add offered\add* by the Israelite people, and they mustn’t profane my holy reputation. I am Yahweh.
\v 3 Tell them that throughout the generations, any of your descendants who is ‘unclean’ and approaches things that have become holy because the people have consecrated them to Yahweh, that person will be cut off from me. I am Yahweh.
\p
\v 4 Any man who’s a descendant of Aaron, if he has a skin disease or a discharge, he mustn’t eat any of the sacred food until he’s ‘clean’ \add again\add*. The same applies if he’s touched any ‘unclean’ corpse, anyone who’s had a seminal emission,
\v 5 anyone who’s touched any swarming creature that makes him ‘unclean’ or touches \add another\add* person who makes him ‘unclean’ (whatever that person’s uncleanness is).
\v 6 \add No,\add* the man who’s touched any of those will be ‘unclean’ until the evening and he mustn’t eat any of the sacred food until he’s washed his body with water.
\v 7 When the sun sets, then he’ll be ‘clean’ and will be able to eat any of those sacred offerings because that’s his food.
\v 8 But he must never eat an animal found dead or torn \add by wild animals\add* and become ‘unclean’ in that way. I am Yahweh.
\p
\v 9 The priests must follow my instructions so they won’t have to bear their own disobedience and die from it because they profaned them. I am Yahweh who makes them sacred.
\p
\v 10 However, no stranger may eat the sacred food, \add e.g.,\add* someone staying with the priest, or his hired servant.
\v 11 If the priest had purchased the person with cash, then that person can eat it, plus anyone that was born into the priest’s household.
\v 12 A priest’s daughter who is married by someone who’s not a priest can \add no longer\add* eat those sacred offerings.
\v 13 \add However, in the future\add* if she became a widow or was divorced without having had children, and if she has returned to her father’s house like when she was young, then she can eat the same food as her father, but no stranger may eat it.
\p
\v 14 If someone accidentally eats something that’s sacred, then they must pay back the full value plus 20% to the priest.
\p
\v 15 So \add in summary\add*, the priests must not profane the sacred things \add contributed by\add* the Israelites and which they’ve offered to Yahweh
\v 16 or else they’ll cause themselves to bear the guilt from their disobedience concerning eating sacred food, because I am Yahweh who has separated them to be sacred.
\p
\v 17 The Yahweh told Moses:
\p
\v 18 Tell Aaron and his sons, and all the Israelites that any Israelite or outsider residing in Israel, when they present their burnt offering to Yahweh, whether it’s for a vow or for a freewill offering,
\v 19 to be accepted it must be a male with no defects, whether it’s a cow, or a sheep, or a goat.
\v 20 Anything that has a defect must \em not\em* be presented because it wouldn’t be acceptable on your behalf.
\v 21 When someone presents a sacrifice of peace offerings to Yahweh to fulfill a vow or as a freewill offering from the herd or the flock, to be accepted it must flawless with no defects—
\v 22 not blind or with broken \add bones\add*, and not maimed or with an oozing sore or rash or scab. You all mustn’t present anything like that to Yahweh, and must not \add even\add* offering any part of them to Yahweh on the altar.
\v 23 However, if a cow or a sheep has something enlarged or something stunted, it can be offered as a freewill offering, but it wouldn’t be accepted for a vow,
\v 24 and you all must never present to Yahweh any animal that’s bruised or torn or cut.\f + \fr 22:24 \ft The Septuagint mentions ‘testicles’ here, which also correlates with 21:20.\f* You mustn’t do that in your country.
\p
\v 25 You all mustn’t present any of those \add animals raised\add* by foreigners to your god because they’ve been ruined and are defective,\f + \fr 22:25 \ft Could this be related to the fact that they’d been neutered (as per v24 in the Septuagint)???\f* so they won’t be accepted for you.
\p
\v 26 Then Yahweh told Moses:
\p
\v 27 A \add newborn\add* calf or lamb or goat must remain with its mother for seven days—it can be offered as a sacrifice to Yahweh from the eighth day onwards,
\v 28 but you mustn’t slaughter a cow or a sheep and its offspring on the same day.
\v 29 When you offer a thanksgiving sacrifice to Yahweh, it must be acceptable as a sacrifice
\v 30 and you can eat it that same day \add but\add* no part of it should be left until the morning. I am Yahweh.
\p
\v 31 You all must record my instructions and follow them. I am Yahweh.
\v 32 You mustn’t profane the reputation of my holiness, and I will demonstrate my holiness among the Israelite people. I am Yahweh who separates you all to be sacred—
\v 33 who brought you all out of Egypt to be your god. I am Yahweh.
\c 23
\s1 The scheduled festivals
\p
\v 1 Then Yahweh told Moses:
\p
\v 2 Tell the \add Israelites\add* that they must declare Yahweh’s scheduled festivals as sacred assemblies at the given times.
\s2 The rest day
\p
\v 3 Work for six days, then the seventh is a rest day \add for\add* a sacred assembly—don’t work on that day—wherever you live it’s a rest day to honour Yahweh.
\p
\v 4 The following are the scheduled festivals that you must declare at the appointed times as sacred assemblies:
\s2 Passover and flat bread festivals
\r (Num. 28:16-25)
\p
\v 5 The 14th of the first month \add (Nisan = March/April) each year\add* at twilight is the \add start of the celebration to honour\add* Yahweh with the ‘passover’.
\p
\v 6 Then the \add next day\add* (the 15th) is \add the start of\add* the festival of flat bread \add to honour\add* Yahweh—you all must eat bread made without any rising agent for seven days.
\v 7 On that first day you’ll hold a sacred assembly—no regular work may be done \add that day\add*.
\v 8 For seven days you all must present offerings to Yahweh, then the seventh day is \add another\add* sacred assembly—\add again\add* no regular work may be done \add that day\add*.
\s1 The firstfruits festival
\p
\v 9 Then Yahweh told Moses:
\p
\v 10 Tell the Israelites that when you enter the land that I’m giving you all, and you harvest your grain crops, then you all must bring a sheaf of the first \add part\add* of your harvest to a priest,
\v 11 and he will wave the sheaf in front of Yahweh to be accepted for you. That must be done the day after the rest day.
\v 12 That same day, you all must offer a year-old lamb with no defects to Yahweh as a burnt offering,
\v 13 and the \add associated\add* grain offering will be two kilograms of fine wheat flour mixed with \add olive\add* oil. That will be a gift to Yahweh with a pleasing aroma, and the \add associated\add* drink offering will be a litre of wine.
\v 14 You all may not eat any bread, or roasted or fresh grain, on that day, until \add after\add* you’ve taken the offering to your god. That is a rule that will continue throughout your generations no matter where you live.
\s1 The fiftieth day festival
\r (Num. 28:26-31)
\p
\v 15 Then count seven rest days from the day after you brought the bundle of grain as a wave offering—seven full weeks.
\v 16 Then that next day \add will make\add* fifty days, and then you all must present a new grain offering to Yahweh.
\v 17 From your homes, each family must bring two loaves of bread baked with a rising agent, as a firstfruits \add offering\add* to Yahweh.
\v 18 Along with the bread, you all must present seven year-old male lambs without defects, one young bull, and two rams. They will be a burnt offering to Yahweh, with their \add associated\add* grain and drink offerings—a gift with a pleasing aroma for Yahweh.
\v 19 You all must \add also\add* offer a male goat as a sin offering and two one-year-old male lambs as a peace offering.
\v 20 The priest must raise them along with the firstfruits bread as a wave offering in front of Yahweh, with the two lambs. They will become sacred to Yahweh, \add but\add* for the priest \add to eat\add*.
\v 21 You all must declare a sacred assembly that same day—you mustn’t do any regular work. That is a permanent rule that will continue throughout the generations no matter where you live.
\p
\v 22 When you all harvest your crops, don’t cut the stalks to the very edge of your field and don’t pick up stalks that drop on the ground—they must be left for the poor and for foreigners \add without land\add*. I am your god, Yahweh.
\s1 The new year festival
\r (Num. 29:1-6)
\p
\v 23 Then Yahweh told Moses,
\v 24 “Tell the Israelites that on the 1st of the seventh month \add (Tishrei = September/October) each year\add*, you must have a public holiday with a loud \add trumpet\add* blast announcing the sacred assembly.
\v 25 You all must not do any regular work on that day, and you all must present a fire-offering to Yahweh.”
\s1 The day of atonement
\r (Num. 29:7-11)
\p
\v 26 Then Yahweh told Moses,
\v 27 “The 10th of that seventh month is a day for making things right \add with Yahweh\add*. You all must hold a sacred assembly and humble yourselves and present a fire-offering to Yahweh.
\v 28 You mustn’t do any work on that particular day because it’s the day for making yourselves right in front of your god Yahweh,
\v 29 because anyone who doesn’t humble themself on that day will be cut off from their people.
\v 30 \add In addition,\add* I will destroy anyone who works on that day—removing them from their people.
\v 31 You all must not do any work. That’s a permanent rule that will continue throughout the generations no matter where you live—
\v 32 it’s a very strict rest day and you all must humble yourselves from the evening of the 9th until the next evening when it will finish.
\s1 The celebration in shelters
\r (Num. 29:12-40)
\p
\v 33 Then Yahweh told Moses:
\p
\v 34 Tell the Israelites that \add starting\add* on the 15th of the seventh month \add (of Tishrei—around September/October)\add*, there will be a celebration in shelters to \add honour\add* Yahweh.
\v 35 The first day will be a sacred assembly—you all mustn’t do any regular work.
\v 36 You all must present a gift to Yahweh \add each day\add* for seven days, and then on the eighth day, there’ll be \add another\add* sacred assembly and you’ll all present a gift to Yahweh. It’ll be \add a day\add* to assemble—you all mustn’t do any regular work \add that day\add*.
\p
\v 37 The above are the scheduled dates to declare sacred assemblies to present a gift to Yahweh: burnt offerings and grain offerings, sacrifices and drink offerings, on the appropriate days,
\v 38 as well as Yahweh’s rest days, and alongside all your \add regular\add* gifts and vows and freewill offerings that you all give to Yahweh.
\p
\v 39 So indeed, on the 15th of the seventh month when you have harvested your crops, you’ll all celebrate Yahweh’s festival for seven days with the first and eighth days being rest days.
\v 40 On that first day, you’ll all bring the fruit from your trees, along with palm branches, leafy branches, and willow branches from \add beside\add* the creeks, and you’ll all happily celebrate in front of your god Yahweh for seven days.
\v 41 Do that for seven days each year as a festival to \add honour\add* Yahweh. you all must celebrate it in the seventh month. That’s a permanent rule that will continue throughout the generations.
\v 42 You all must live in shelters for seven days—all you native-born Israelites,
\v 43 so that the \add future\add* generations will know that I made their ancestors live in shelters when I brought them out of Egypt. I am your god Yahweh.
\p
\v 44 So Moses passed all those scheduled dates from Yahweh, on to the Israelites.
\c 24
\s1 Oil for the lamps
\r (Exo. 27:20-21)
\p
\v 1 Then Yahweh told Moses:
\p
\v 2 Order the Israelites to bring you pressed olive oil that’s pure, to supply lamps \add to give\add* continual light.
\v 3 \add The high priest\add* (Aaron must take care of them from evening until the morning \add to burn\add* continually in front of Yahweh. That’s a permanent requirement that will continue throughout the generations.
\v 4 He will arrange the lamps continually on the lampstand in front of Yahweh.
\s1 The bread offered there to God
\p
\v 5 \add Each week\add* you must take fine wheat flour and bake twelve loaves of bread, using two kilograms of flour for each loaf.
\v 6 Arrange the loaves into two rows of six loaves on the pure table in front of Yahweh,
\v 7 and put pure frankincense with each row to be a memorial portion for the bread as a gift to Yahweh.
\v 8 \add Every\add* rest day it will be placed in front of Yahweh \add representing\add* the ongoing agreement with the Israelites.
\v 9 Afterwards, it will belong to Aaron and his sons, and they must eat it in a sacred area because it’s a very sacred part of the gifts for Yahweh. That’s a permanent rule.
\s1 Execution for cursing Yahweh
\p
\v 10 Once there was an Israelite woman and an Egyptian man who had a son who lived among the Israelites, and he got into a fight with an Israelite man.
\v 11 Then that son of the Israelite woman slandered Yahweh’s name and cursed, and \add so\add* the people took him to Moses. (The mother was Shelomit, a daughter of Divri from the Dan tribe.)
\v 12 They held him in custody while waiting for Yahweh’s decision.
\p
\v 13 Then Yahweh told Moses:
\p
\v 14 Take that man who cursed outside the camp, and everyone who heard him must place their hands on his head, and then all the people must execute him by throwing rocks at him.
\v 15 Then tell the people that anyone who curses God must bear \add the results of\add* their own wickedness.
\v 16 Anyone who slanders Yahweh’s reputation must be executed. The \add gathered assembly\add* must throw rocks at them. \add It makes no difference\add* whether they’re a foreigner staying there, or a native-born \add Israelite\add*.
\p
\v 17 Anyone who murders another person, must definitely be executed.
\v 18 Anyone who kills \add someone else’s\add* animal, must make restitution with the same kind of animal.
\p
\v 19 Anyone who injures a fellow citizen, must have the same done to them—
\v 20 fracture for fracture, eye for eye, tooth for tooth—however they injured the other person, that’s what must be done to them.
\v 21 Anyone who kills \add someone else’s\add* animal must replace it, but anyone who kills \add another\add* person must be executed.
\v 22 There’s \add only\add* one \add set of\add* regulations for \add both\add* the foreigner and the native-born \add citizen\add* because I am your god Yahweh.
\p
\v 23 Moses passed \add all that\add* onto the people, and they took the man who had cursed \add Yahweh\add* outside the camp where they executed him with rocks. The Israelites did exactly what Yahweh had commanded \add them\add* via Moses.
\c 25
\s1 The rest year
\r (Deu. 15:1-11)
\p
\v 1 Then on Mt. Sinai, Yahweh told Moses:
\p
\v 2 Tell the Israelites that when you all enter the land that I’m giving you, the land must \add observe\add* a rest year for Yahweh.
\v 3 You can plant your crops, prune your grapevines, and harvest \add all\add* its produce for six years,
\v 4 but during the seventh year, you must allow the land to rest \add to honour\add* Yahweh. You mustn’t plant any crops or prune any grapevines.
\v 5 Don’t \add systematically\add* harvest the self-sown crops or the grapes from the untrimmed vines. It must be a rest year for the land.
\v 6 However, during the rest year, the land will provide food for you and your servants, and for your hired workers and the foreigners who are living among you,
\v 7 as well as your livestock and the \add wild\add* animals across the land. Any produce from the land can be eaten.
\s1 The restoration celebration year
\p
\v 8 Count off seven rest years, so seven times seven which is forty-nine years,
\v 9 then \add after that\add*, sound loud horn blasts on the 10th of the seventh month to announce throughout the land that it’s the day for making things right with Yahweh.
\v 10 Then you all must declare that fiftieth year to be sacred. It’ll be a restoration celebration \add year\add* when you announce freedom for all the inhabitants of the country—everyone can be free to return to their families, and \add all families can be free\add* to return to their originally inherited land.
\v 11 That fiftieth year will be a restoration celebration year. You all mustn’t plant crops and don’t \add systematically\add* harvest the self-sown crops or the grapes from the untrimmed vines,
\v 12 because it’s a restoration celebration year. You all must \add treat it\add* as sacred and \add just\add* eat what you take \add directly\add* from the countryside.
\p
\v 13 In that restoration celebration year, you all should return to your inherited land.
\v 14 \add In the other years\add* when you buy or sell from your fellow citizens, you mustn’t cheat them
\v 15 \add because the value of the land\add* will depend on the number of years after the restoration celebration year, \add i.e.,\add* you must take into account the number of years of produce—
\v 16 if there’s many years \add to go\add*, the price will increase, and if the next rest year is close, the price will decrease because you’re selling the number of crops.
\v 17 So don’t cheat eat other—you must respect and obey your god, because I am your god Yahweh.
\p
\v 18 You all must follow all my instructions and maintain my values—keeping them so that you’ll be able to live securely on the land.
\v 19 The land will yield its crops and you’ll all have plenty to eat, and you’ll be able to live there securely.
\p
\v 20 But if you all wonder what you’ll eat in that seventh year when you’ll neither plant crops or do organised harvests,
\v 21 well I’ll send my blessing to you all in the sixth year, and it’ll produce enough for three years
\v 22 so you all can still eat that stored food while you plant crops in the eighth year to feed you all through the ninth year.
\s1 Returning property to the clan
\p
\v 23 The land can not be irrevocably sold, because it belongs to me and you all are foreigners and temporary residents on my \add land\add*.
\v 24 so for all the land that you take possession of, there must be a way to get back the family land.
\v 25 \add For example,\add* if an Israelite man becomes poor and sells off some of his inherited land, then a close relative \add must have the right to\add* go and buy back that land.
\v 26 Even if he didn’t have a relative who could do that, and he prospers \add again\add* \add in the future\add* and is able to raise enough money,
\v 27 then he can calculate the value of the years \add until the restoration celebration\add*, and then for that amount he must be allowed to buy the land back from the person he’d sold it to.
\v 28 However, if the original owner wasn’t able to raise enough money, then that piece of land that was sold will remain under the control of the buyer until the restoration celebration year when it must be returned \add anyway\add*.
\p
\v 29 If a house inside a walled city is sold, then the right to buy it back will last until one full year after the sale.
\v 30 If it’s not bought back \add by the original family\add* within that year, then that house in the walled city will then belong permanently to the buyer—it won’t be returned in the restoration celebration year.
\v 31 On the contrary, village houses will be considered as part of the family land, so they’ll be able to be bought back \add by the family\add*, otherwise they’ll be returned in the restoration celebration year.
\p
\v 32 The Levites’ cities and houses will belong to them permanently—the Levites must \+em always\+em* be able to buy them back,
\v 33 but \add even\add* if they \add don’t\add* buy them back, they must be returned in the restoration celebration year because the Levites’ houses are their \add only\add* possession among the Israelites.
\v 34 In addition, they may not sell the pastureland around their cities because that’s their permanent possession.
\s1 Assisting the poor
\p
\v 35 If a fellow citizen becomes poor and is unable to survive beside you, then you must support that person and allow him to live with you like a foreigner might.
\v 36 You mustn’t charge that person any interest or make a profit from him, but you must respect and obey your god, and your fellow citizen can \add continue\add* to live with you.
\v 37 If you loan him money, don’t charge interest or make a profit from his food.
\v 38 I am your god Yahweh who brought you all out of Egypt to give you the Canaan region \add and\add* to be your god.
\s1 Treatment of slaves
\p
\v 39 If a fellow citizen becomes poor and selling himself to you, don’t force him to work like a slave—
\v 40 treat him like a hired worker or a temporary resident. He can work with you until the restoration celebration year
\v 41 when he must be released along with his children, and he can return to his family, and to his clan’s land,
\v 42 because they are my slaves that I brought out of Egypt so they mustn’t be sold \add again\add* as slaves.
\v 43 Don’t rule harshly over them but rather, respect and obey your god.
\v 44 If you want to have male or female slaves, they you can buy them from the surrounding countries,
\v 45 or else from the children or clans of the foreigners who are living among you—you all can own them
\v 46 and then pass them on to your children to own as their inheritance. You all can make them work indefinitely \add for you\add*, but you mustn’t harshly oppress your fellow Israelites.
\p
\v 47 If a foreigner living among you becomes wealthy, yet your fellow citizen becomes poor and sells himself to that resident foreigner or to a member of their clan,
\v 48 then there’s always the right of redemption even after he’s sold himself, \add so\add* one of his brothers can buy him back,
\v 49 or his uncle or cousin or another relative from the clan can pay to free him. In fact, he might even be able to buy his own way out.
\v 50 He must work out with his master how many years from when he sold himself, until the restoration celebration year, and figure out the price according to the wages of a hired worker for that many years.
\v 51 If there’s still a lot of years to go, he’d need to repay most of what he sold himself for,
\v 52 but if it’s only a few years to go, the price for his freedom would be proportionally smaller.
\v 53 While he works for the foreign master, he must be treated like an annual hired worker. You others must ensure that he’s not treated harshly.
\v 54 Then even if he’s not bought out, he and his children must be released in the restoration celebration year
\v 55 because the Israelites are my slaves—my slaves because I brought them out of Egypt. I am your god Yahweh.
\c 26
\s1 Obedience will lead to prosperity
\r (Deu. 7:12-24; 28:1-14)
\p
\v 1 You all mustn’t make worthless idols or carved images for yourselves, or set up sacred pillars. In your country, you must never bow down to an \add engraved\add* stone image, because I am your god Yahweh.
\v 2 You all must observe my rest days and honour my sanctuary. I am Yahweh.
\p
\v 3 If you’ll all follow my instructions and remember my rules and obey them,
\v 4 then I’ll give you all rain in the proper season, and the land will produce crops and the trees in the countryside will produce fruit.
\v 5 The \add grain\add* threshing will continue until the grape harvest, and the grape harvesting will continue until it’s time to plant new crops, and so you’ll all have plenty of food and you’ll live securely \add there\add* on your land.
\p
\v 6 Then I’ll give peace in your country so that you’ll all sleep without being afraid. I’ll remove the wild animals from your area and there’ll be no raids or wars in your region.
\v 7 You’ll all \add be able to\add* chase away your enemies and destroy them—
\v 8 five of you will chase away a hundred, and a hundred of you will chase away ten thousand, and you’ll be able to defeat your enemies.
\p
\v 9 I’ll give you all my attention and bless you with many children, and I’ll maintain my agreement with you all.
\v 10 You’ll all \add still\add* be eating the previous year’s grain when the new harvest starts, and you’ll have to move out that left-over grain \add to make room for the new\add*.
\v 11 Then I’ll put my residence among you all and won’t reject you,
\v 12 and I’ll walk among you and be your god, and you’ll all be my people.
\v 13 I am your god Yahweh who brought you all out from being slaves in Egypt, and I broke the chains off you so that you all could walk with heads erect.
\s1 Punishments for disobedience
\r (Deu. 28:15-68)
\p
\v 14 However, if you all won’t listen to me and won’t follow all those instructions,
\v 15 and if you all reject my regulations and my values to the point of hating my commands, thus breaking my agreement,
\v 16 then this is what I’ll do to you all: I’ll send sudden terror to you all, and wasting diseases and sicknesses. You’ll all be wasting your time sowing seeds because your enemies will eat the harvest.
\v 17 I’ll take action against you, and you’ll be defeated by your enemies. You’ll be ruled over by those who hate you, and you’ll all run away frightened \add even\add* when no one’s chasing you.
\p
\v 18 If you all \add still\add* won’t listen to me after that, then I’ll continue on to discipline you seven times over for your disobedience.
\v 19 Then I’ll smash your strong pride, and I’ll make the sky like iron \add with no rain\add* and your land \add hard\add* like bronze.
\v 20 Your energy spent working your land will be \add just\add* wasted as your land won’t produce its crops, and the trees won’t produce any fruit.
\p
\v 21 Then if you’re all \add still\add* being contrary and unwilling to listen to me, then I’ll send seven times more deadly diseases according to your disobedience.
\v 22 Then I’ll send in the wild animals to make you childless and to cut back your livestock, thus diminishing your numbers and \add leaving\add* your roads desolate.
\p
\v 23 Then after that if you all \add still\add* won’t accept my discipline and continue to oppose me,
\v 24 then I myself will oppose you all and punish you all seven times over for your disobedience.
\v 25 I’ll send armies against you all to avenge the breaking of our agreement. You’ll be shut into your cities where I’ll send diseases in for you all, and allow your enemies to defeat you.
\v 26 When I limit your food supplies, ten women will bake bread in one oven and it will be rationed by weight—you’ll all eat but never be satisfied.
\p
\v 27 In spite of \add all\add* that, if you all \add still\add* won’t listen to me, and continue to oppose me,
\v 28 then I’ll be in a hostile rage against you all and I’ll discipline you seven times \add more\add* for your disobedience
\v 29 and you’ll \add start\add* eating your own sons and daughters.
\v 30 I’ll destroy your hilltop shrines and cut down you incense altars, then I’ll pile your corpses on top of your idols’ corpses, and I myself will detest you all.
\v 31 I’ll make your cities into heaps of rubble and I’ll devastate your sanctuaries and \add no longer\add* smell the pleasing aromas \add of your sacrifices\add*.
\p
\v 32 I’ll devastate the region, and your enemies who live in it will be appalled.
\v 33 Then I’ll scatter you all among \add other\add* nations and then armies will come in after you and your land will become desolate and your cities will become rubble,
\v 34 and the land will enjoy all its rest times while it’s desolate and you’re all in your enemies’ countries. The land will stop producing and enjoy its times of resting.
\v 35 It will rest all through its desolation since it didn’t get its rest times when you lived on it.
\p
\v 36 Then those of you who are left in your enemies’ countries, I’ll make you paranoid \add there\add*—even the sound of a blowing leaf will make them run away as if someone’s chasing them with a sword, and they’ll fall down even when no one’s chasing them.
\v 37 They’ll stumble over each other as if being attacked, but there won’t be anyone chasing them, and then you all won’t have the energy to stand up against your enemies.
\v 38 You’ll die in \add various\add* countries and your enemies will consume you.
\v 39 Those of you who are left will rot away amongst the wickedness in your enemies’ countries and because of the wickedness of their ancestors.
\p
\v 40 However, if they confess their wickedness and their ancestors’ wickedness, that they’ve disobeyed and neglected me, and been hostile towards me,
\v 41 (and that I became hostile toward them and took their into their enemies’ countries,) and humble their undisciplined hearts and admit their disobedience,
\v 42 then I’ll remember my agreements with Jacob and with Isaac and with Abraham, and I’ll remember this land.
\v 43 The land \add here\add* will have been deserted by them and will have enjoyed its times of rest, and they’ll accept their wickedness because they rejected my values and they really detested my regulations.
\v 44 Despite that, I won’t reject them in their enemies’ countries, and I won’t detest them enough to completely destroy them \add and\add* to break my agreement with them, because I am their god Yahweh.
\v 45 I’ll remember the earlier agreement with their ancestors that I brought out of Egypt while all the nations watched me be their god. I am Yahweh.
\p
\v 46 The above are the instructions and values and rules that Yahweh instilled between him and the Israelites through Moses there at Mt. Sinai.
\c 27
\s1 Valuations of vows to Yahweh
\p
\v 1 Then Yahweh told Moses:
\p
\v 2 Tell the Israelites that when someone makes a special vow to offer someone to Yahweh,
\v 3 a man between twenty and sixty will be valued at fifty silver coins (according to the sanctuary standard weights)
\v 4 and a woman at thirty coins.
\v 5 If it’s someone between five and twenty, then it’s twenty silver coins for a male and ten for a female.
\v 6 Between one month old and five years, it’s five silver coins for a male and three for a female.
\v 7 For sixty years old and older, it’s fifteen silver coins for a male and ten for a female.
\p
\v 8 If the maker of the vow can’t afford the valuation, then that person must stand in front of the priest \add and give an account\add*, and then the priest can set a value that they can afford.
\p
\v 9 If it’s an animal that they’re promising as a gift for Yahweh, then that animal becomes sacred—
\v 10 it can’t be exchanged for a different one (either better or worse), but if it does get substituted, then both animals will become sacred.
\v 11 Any ‘unclean’ animal cannot be presented to Yahweh, \add so if that’s what was promised\add*, the person must take the animal to the priest,
\v 12 and a priest must give a fair valuation according to the quality of the animal—the priest’s decision will be final.
\v 13 However, if it’s later bought back, then an extra twenty percent must be added to that valuation.
\p
\v 14 If someone dedicates their house as sacred to Yahweh, a priest must value it according to its quality—the priest’s decision will be final.
\v 15 However, if that person \add decides to\add* buy back their house, then an extra twenty percent must be added to the valuation and then it can be theirs again.
\p
\v 16 If someone dedicates part of their farmland as sacred to Yahweh, then it will be valued according to how much seed \add would be sown in it\add*—fifty silver coins for each 130 kilograms of barley \add seed required\add*.
\v 17 That would be its value if it was dedicated during the restoration celebration year,
\v 18 but in the years following, the priest must calculate the field’s value depending on how many years remain before the next rest year.
\v 19 However, if that person \add decides to\add* buy back their land, then an extra twenty percent must be added to the valuation and then it can be theirs again.
\v 20 If the person doesn’t buy back the field but sells it to someone else, then it can’t be bought back again.
\v 21 Then when the field is released at the next rest year, it will be sacred to Yahweh and the priests will take ownership.
\p
\v 22 If someone dedicates to Yahweh some land that they’ve bought (i.e., it’s not part of their \add clan\add* inheritance),
\v 23 then a priest must calculate the valuation until the \add next\add* restoration celebration year, and it must be paid that same day —becoming sacred to Yahweh.
\v 24 In that rest year, the land will return to the original owner who has it as part of their clan inheritance.
\p
\v 25 All valuations will be calculated using the standard weights from the sanctuary—the silver coin must weigh twelve grams.
\p
\v 26 Every firstborn domestic animal (including cows and sheep) \add automatically\add* belongs to Yahweh, and no person can \add claim to\add* ‘dedicate’ it.
\v 27 However, if it’s an ‘unclean’ animal then it must be bought back by paying its valuation plus an additional twenty percent, but if it’s not bought back, then it must be sold at its valuation \add price\add*.
\p
\v 28 If someone dedicates something to Yahweh that they own (e.g., a man or an animal, or a field), they can’t then sell it or buy it \add straight\add* back—everything that’s dedicated \add like that\add* becomes sacred to Yahweh.
\p
\v 29 Any person who’s dedicated for destruction cannot be bought back—they must be executed.
\p
\v 30 A tenth of all the harvests from the crops and fruit from the trees, belongs to Yahweh. It’s sacred to Yahweh.
\v 31 If someone \add really\add* wanted to buy back their tenth \add of their produce\add*, \add an additional\add* twenty percent must be added \add to the valuation\add*.
\v 32 Every tenth animal of the mustered domestic cattle and sheep and goats, is sacred to Yahweh.
\v 33 The herder mustn’t pick out good or bad ones, or make substitutions. (If a substitution was made, both animals then become sacred and must not be bought back.)
\p
\v 34 The above are the instructions and rules that Yahweh gave Moses at Mt. Sinai for the Israelites.
